    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34688": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist - AI-Enabled Immune Digital Twin &amp; Organ Damage Mechanistic Model",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Scientist - AI-Enabled Immune Digital Twin &amp; Organ Damage Mechanistic Model Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Scientist - AI-Enabled Immune Digital Twin & Organ Damage Mechanistic Model Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Scientist - AI-Enabled Immune Digital Twin & Organ Damage Mechanistic Model Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Intelligent Systems Engineering Location Bloomington Position Summary AI-Enabled Immune Digital Twin & Organ damage Mechanistic Model Scientist: Assistant Scientist; Biocomplexity Institute at Indiana University Bloomington (PI: Dr. James A. Glazier) Your primary duties will be to develop biologically meaningful, interpretable model components linking immune-state dynamics to organ-damage risk (lung, kidney, liver) in critical illness under the direction of Dr. James A. Glazier. You will map clinical biomarkers to model states, define biological scoring criteria for digital twin ensembles, collaborate with national consortium partners, write technical publications, and prepare milestone-ready contract deliverables. 60% Dynamic Immune & Organ Modeling: Develop fast, interpretable ODE /network/mechanistic model modules linking immune dysfunction to organ injury; map clinical time-series and biomarkers to model states; formulate biological plausibility and scoring criteria. 40% Consortium Deliverables & Research: Prepare test cases, model packages, and model cards; write research papers; present findings; collaborate with national consortium partners in systems biology and clinical data science. Basic Qualifications Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in quantitative, computational, or biomedical discipline. Programming proficiency in Python, Julia, R, or MATLAB ; background in mechanistic modeling ( ODE /dynamical systems, QSP , or agent-based); familiarity with inflammation, cytokine signaling, sepsis, or critical care biology. One year of successful postdoctoral research experience. Department Contact for Questions Please contact Professor James Glazier at glazier@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank 100,000/Assistant Scientist Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/02/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101731-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
