    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34679": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow (Bacterial Sulfur Metabolism) Giedroc Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow (Bacterial Sulfur Metabolism) Giedroc Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow (Bacterial Sulfur Metabolism) Giedroc Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow (Bacterial Sulfur Metabolism) Giedroc Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Chemistry Location Bloomington Position Summary Department Information: The Department of Chemistry in the College of Arts and Sciences at Indiana University Bloomington, consists of a well-rounded research community with cutting-edge expertise in a wide variety of modern disciplines including analytical chemistry, organic chemistry, inorganic chemistry, physical chemistry, materials chemistry, and chemical biology and biochemistry. The chemical biology area of the Department of Chemistry is located in Simon Hall, home to our graduate training program in Quantitative and Chemical Biology and nearby other biochemists and structural biologists and all structural biology and analytical chemistry core facilities. Physical proximity to microbiologists in the Department of Biology (across the courtyard) and weekly participation in the Microphiles trainee-led seminar series held in Simon Hall promotes strong interactions with these groups as well. Job Summary: Prof. Giedroc seeks a postdoctoral research associate as a member of multidisciplinary team of students and Ph.D.-level staff tasked with obtaining a molecular understanding bacterial sulfur metabolism and mechanisms of hydrogen sulfide (H2S)/reactive sulfur species ( RSS ) homeostasis in strict anaerobes. We seek to appoint a postdoctoral trainee to conduct microbiological, biochemical and structural studies of proteins involved in bacterial sulfur metabolism and hydrogen sulfide (H2S)/reactive sulfur species ( RSS ) homeostasis. This project benefits strongly from long-standing pathogenesis collaborators at the Vanderbilt University Medical Center. The position is supported by an existing NIH grant to Prof. Giedroc, and support is provided for up to three years pending satisfactory research progress by the successful candidate. Basic Qualifications Responsibilities: The successful candidate is expected to take a leadership position in the laboratory, driving a project forward, working largely independently, alongside professional staff collaborators on the Bloomington campus as needed. This individual will be responsible for all aspects of their project, from project design in collaboration with Prof. Giedroc, to execution of experiments, and analysis of all data acquired. The successful candidate will help to co-author manuscripts for publication. This individual will have an opportunity to attend one scientific conference annually to present the results of their project, and to build a professional network in the US. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in microbiology, biochemistry, chemical biology or a related discipline, with no postdoctoral experience necessary at the time of appointment. Preferred Qualifications: The preferred candidate will have experience in all modern microbiological methods, and a strong interest in learning recombinant protein purification and associated biochemical methods, and/or core techniques of bioanalytical chemistry (mass spectrometry) routinely used in the laboratory. The successful candidate will possess excellent communication skills and a strong outcomes orientation. $57,500/year, Postdoctoral Fellow The initial appointment for this position will be for 12 months. Additional 12-month extensions will be available based on performance. A competitive benefits package will also be offered. Special Instructions: Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34129 . Applications will be reviewed upon receipt. Letters of recommendation will be requested upon receipt of the application. Best for Consideration Date (3 weeks from posting date): September 21, 2026 Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to: Prof. David P. Giedroc, Department of Chemistry, Indiana University, 800 E. Kirkwood Avenue, Bloomington, IN 47405, giedroc@iu.edu. For additional information visit: https://giedroc.lab.iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/21/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101644-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34678": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Dharan Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Dharan Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Dharan Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Dharan Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The Dharan Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University, Bloomington is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral associate to work on HIV -1 related projects with minimal supervision. The primary goal of the lab is to characterize the role of nuclear pore complex machinery in mediating HIV -1 nuclear entry and subsequent downstream process that leads to productive viral gene integration. The goal of this position is to work on active projects to generate data for publications and to also generate data for external funding. The prospective candidate should be willing to work in a collaborative environment, assisting graduate students, supervising undergraduate students and presenting their work in shared lab meetings and outside meetings. Basic Qualifications Education and Experience required: Highly motivated individuals holding a Ph.D. degree in virology and preferably on HIV -1 research are encouraged to apply. The lab combines basic virology and cell biology approaches including widefield, confocal and super resolution imaging techniques and flow cytometry to address key questions. The prospective candidate should have the knowledge to perform cell culture works and a willingness to learn and trouble shoot new techniques. Required skills: PhD in Virology · Cell culture · Viral vector production · Viral infection assays · Basic cloning techniques · Microscopy (widefield or confocal) techniques · Flow cytometry · CRISPR Knockout and knock in techniques · Ability to plot and interpret data and come up with follow up experiments · Demonstrates mastery of time management · Maintains a high degree of professionalism · Ability to work as a team · Proficient communication skills, written and oral · Anticipates and solves problems · High degree of initiative Preferred skills: PhD in Virology with HIV research · Image analysis techniques · Mass Spectrometry techniques Department Contact for Questions To apply , please submit (1) a cover letter describing your current research and educational background, your career goals, why you think you are suitable for this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV or resume and (3) the name and contact info of two references. Application and documents can be submitted at this link: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34053 . For questions about the position, contact Adarsh Dharan ( adharan@iu.edu ). Timing and compensation: Review of applicants will begin soon and continue until this position is filled. The ideal start date is November 1st, 2026. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment is contingent upon satisfactory performance. Salary will be commensurate with experience, and full benefits are included. Best consideration date is 9/1/26. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/01/2026 Expected Start Date 11/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101633-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34653": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Post Doctoral Fellow Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Post Doctoral Fellow Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This is a two-year, full-time position housed in the Department of Geography at Indiana University Bloomington. The postdoctoral scholar will be based at Indiana University and jointly mentored by Dr. Darren Ficklin at Indiana University Bloomington and Dr. Margaret Zimmer at the University of Wisconsin–Madison. Posting Details Position Details Title Post Doctoral Fellow Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Geography Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Geography at Indiana University Bloomington invites applications for a Postdoctoral Scholar to conduct independent research under the joint mentorship of Dr. Darren Ficklin (Indiana University Bloomington) and Dr. Margaret Zimmer (University of Wisconsin–Madison). The postdoctoral scholar will be based in the Department of Geography at Indiana University Bloomington and will have opportunities to interact and collaborate with research groups at both Indiana University and the University of Wisconsin–Madison. This position provides an opportunity for an early-career researcher to develop and lead an independent research project broadly related to the complementary research interests of Drs. Ficklin and Zimmer. Rather than being tied to a predetermined research project, the position is designed to support creative, independent scholarship. Applicants are asked to propose a research project that they would pursue during the postdoctoral appointment. Dr. Ficklin’s research broadly focuses on hydrology and hydroclimatology, including climate change impacts on hydrologic systems, hydrologic modeling, streamflow and stream temperature, and large-scale computational approaches. Dr. Zimmer’s research focuses on watershed hydrology, including water quantity and quality, surface water–groundwater interactions, non-perennial streams, and soil water, with a strong emphasis on field-based investigation. Together, these areas provide opportunities for research spanning field observations, process-based hydrology, hydroclimatology, and computational and modeling approaches. Broad areas of potential interest include, but are not limited to: · climate change impacts on hydrologic systems; · watershed and catchment hydrology; · hydroclimatology and climate–water interactions; · surface water–groundwater interactions; · non-perennial streams and hydrologic connectivity; · soil water and subsurface hydrologic processes; · water quantity and water quality; · streamflow and stream temperature; · hydrologic and environmental modeling; · field-based hydrologic and environmental research; · extreme hydroclimatic events; · large-scale or computational hydrology; · climate and hydrologic data analysis; and · related questions at the intersection of climate, water, and environmental change. Computational and modeling project ideas are all encouraged, particularly projects that creatively integrate observations with modeling, data science, remote sensing, or other quantitative approaches. Applicants are not expected to propose work identical to existing projects in either research group. Rather, proposed projects should take advantage of the complementary expertise of Drs. Ficklin and Zimmer while allowing the postdoctoral scholar to establish a distinct research direction. The successful candidate will have substantial independence in developing and carrying out their research agenda while being an active member of the Ficklin Lab at Indiana University. The postdoctoral scholar will work alongside Ph.D. and undergraduate students, participate in lab meetings and research activities, and contribute to a collaborative research environment. Regular interaction and collaboration with Dr. Zimmer and her research group at the University of Wisconsin–Madison will also be encouraged. Opportunities will additionally exist to develop collaborations within the Department of Geography and across Indiana University and the University of Wisconsin–Madison. Basic Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Geography, Hydrology, Climate Science, Earth or Environmental Science, Atmospheric Science, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Competitive applicants will demonstrate: · A strong record of research appropriate to their career stage. · Evidence of the ability to conduct independent research. · Experience with quantitative, computational, and/or observational research methods appropriate to their area of expertise. · Strong written and oral communication skills. · The ability and interest to work both independently and collaboratively within a research group. · Strong scientific programming skills. Research Proposal As part of the application, applicants should submit a two-page research proposal describing the project they would pursue during the postdoctoral appointment. The proposal should address: 1. The primary research question(s) and their scientific significance. 2. The proposed methodological approach, including any experiments, datasets, observations, models, or computational resources that would be required. 3. The anticipated research products or outcomes. 4. How the proposed work connects with or complements the research expertise of Drs. Ficklin and Zimmer and how the project could benefit from their complementary expertise. 5. How the project would contribute to the applicant’s longer-term independent research trajectory. The proposal should be sufficiently focused to demonstrate that the project is feasible within the two-year postdoctoral appointment, while recognizing that the research plan may evolve through discussions with Drs. Ficklin and Zimmer after the scholar begins the position. Applicants are encouraged to propose ambitious and innovative projects. Proposed projects need not replicate ongoing research in either research group but should have sufficient intellectual or methodological overlap to benefit from the combined expertise and research environments of the two groups. Department Contact for Questions Applicants with questions regarding the position should contact both Dr. Darren Ficklin (dficklin@iu.edu) and Dr. Margaret Zimmer (margaret.zimmer@wisc.edu). Additional Qualifications Applicants should submit: 1. A cover letter describing their interest in the position, relevant research background, and how the postdoctoral opportunity aligns with their career goals. 2. A curriculum vitae. 3. A two-page research proposal as described above. 4. Two representative publications or manuscripts. Both manuscripts should be under review. 5. Contact information for two professional references. Salary and Rank 60,000 Special Instructions This is a two-year, full-time position housed in the Department of Geography at Indiana University Bloomington. The postdoctoral scholar will be based at Indiana University and jointly mentored by Dr. Darren Ficklin at Indiana University Bloomington and Dr. Margaret Zimmer at the University of Wisconsin–Madison. For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 01/04/2027 Posting Number IU-101736-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Publication 1 Publication 2 Research Agenda List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34652": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellowship",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Research Fellowship Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Research Fellowship Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Research Fellowship Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Mathematics Location Bloomington Position Summary INDIANA UNIVERSITY DEPARTMENT OF MATHEMATICS POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCH FELLOWSHIPS The Department of Mathematics seeks applications for two Postdoctoral Research Fellowships beginning in the Fall of 2027. These are non-tenure track positions with nominal teaching loads of four courses per year and expected 3-year appointments. Outstanding candidates with a recent Ph.D. in any area of pure or applied mathematics are encouraged to apply; a minimum requirement is a Ph.D. in Mathematics. Fellows are paired with mentors with whom they have compatible research interests. The Department maintains strong research groups in all of the principal fields of mathematics. Application Instructions Applicants should submit the following materials using the online service provided by the AMS at http://www.mathjobs.org. If unable to do so, applicants may send application materials to the address below. • AMS cover sheet • curriculum vita • research statement • teaching statement • cover letter Applicants should arrange for four letters of recommendation, including one evaluating teaching experience. Please ask reference writers to submit their letters electronically through http://www.mathjobs.org . If they are unable to do so, they may also send their letters to the following address: Postdoctoral Fellowships Search Committee, Department of Mathematics, Indiana University, 831 East 3rd Street, Rawles Hall, Bloomington, IN 47405-7106. Applicants should then complete the posting at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/31704; no additional documents are required to be uploaded to this link. Applications received by January 21, 2027 will receive full consideration. However, review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. If the applicant has questions or concerns regarding their application, please contact Clay Collier, (812) 855-2200, cldcolli@iu.edu. Equal Opportunity Statement Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. For details, please see the Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination. About Bloomington Located in the forested hills of southern Indiana, Bloomington offers a vibrant community with a rich variety of cultural, musical, and recreational opportunities. Basic Qualifications Outstanding candidates with a recent Ph.D. in any area of pure or applied mathematics are encouraged to apply; a minimum requirement is a Ph.D. in Mathematics. Fellows are paired with mentors with whom they have compatible research interests. Department Contact for Questions Clay Collier Indiana University Department of Mathematics Administrative Services Program Manager 831 E. 3rd St. Rawles Hall 106 Bloomington, IN 47405 (812) 855-2200 Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/21/2027 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number 