    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34601": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Visiting Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Visiting Assistant Professor Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications received by January 4, 2027 will receive full consideration. However, we will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and apply online at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34583 We will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Application Materials: 1) CV 2) Letter of Application that includes a statement of teaching philosophy, documented teaching experience, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. 3) Contact information for at least 3 references from whom we can solicit a letter of recommendation. Posting Details Position Details Title Visiting Assistant Professor Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Psychological & Brain Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary We seek to fill two positions for visiting assistant professors to teach undergraduate courses in one or more of the following areas: Social, Developmental, Cognitive, or Clinical Psychology. The candidate may also contribute to general courses in our Psychology, Neuroscience, or Clinical Science undergraduate degrees, such as introductory psychology, experimental methods, or statistics. This will be a 1-year appointment, renewable for one additional year. The teaching load is five courses per year. Candidates with a doctorate in psychology or related field in hand at the time of appointment are preferred, but those who have completed all degree requirements except the dissertation defense (i.e., ABDs) will be considered. Applicants should have documented teaching experience and submit a letter of application that includes a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, evidence of teaching effectiveness, a curriculum vita, and three referees from whom we can solicit a letter of recommendation. H1-B sponsorship is not available for this position. Bloomington, Indiana is a university town with high quality of life. Bloomington is within a one-hour drive of Indianapolis and a two-hour drive of two other major metropolitan centers. Bloomington also has a low cost of living relative to entry-level faculty salaries. The campus is home to many thriving affinity groups and offers an exceptional selection of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities. Department practices are family-friendly and the university has a flexible remote work policy. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their application at (Link here). Review of applications will begin January 4, 2027 and continue until the positions are filled. The position start date is August 1, 2027 . General information about the department and the university is available at http://psych.indiana.edu . Basic Qualifications Basic Qualifications (Education, Degree, Skills, etc.): PhD (or ABD ) in Psychology or related field Documented teaching experience Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to: Jillian Odle-Araman, Assistant Director of Undergraduate Studies, at jiodle@iu.edu or By mail at ATTN : VAP Search, Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, 1101 E. 10th Street, Bloomington, IN 47405-7007. Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications (Education, Degree, Skills, etc.): PhD (or ABD ) in Psychology or related field Documented teaching experience Salary and Rank Special Instructions Applications received by January 4, 2027 will receive full consideration. However, we will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and apply online at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34583 We will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Application Materials: 1) CV 2) Letter of Application that includes a statement of teaching philosophy, documented teaching experience, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. 3) Contact information for at least 3 references from whom we can solicit a letter of recommendation. For Best Consideration Date 01/04/2027 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101722-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34581": {
      "title": "Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Communication Skills)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Communication Skills) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Communication Skills) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Communication Skills) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills Location Bloomington Position Summary The Kelley School of Business at Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for full-time, open-rank Lecturer positions in Communication Skills beginning Fall 2027. These are 10-month, non-tenure-track appointments with an 18-credit-hour annual teaching assignment. Communication Skills faculty teach core undergraduate courses in business writing and business presentations. The curriculum emphasizes audience, purpose, rhetorical situation, and the effective use of written and oral genres in professional contexts. Faculty typically have a primary assignment in either Business Writing or Business Presentations, with the possibility of teaching both. Communication Skills Courses Business Presentations (3 credits) Business Presentations introduces students to oral communication in professional contexts through theory-based skill development. Students learn to analyze audiences, construct and deliver persuasive messages, develop oral arguments, and work effectively in teams. The course includes a team persuasive presentation addressing a real problem for a corporate client. Business Writing (3 credits) Business Writing develops students’ ability to produce strategic, ethical, audience-centered professional documents individually and in teams. Students analyze communication situations, make rhetorical choices across business genres, and develop recommendations for authentic professional problems. The course includes a team-based report for a corporate client. The Faculty Role Lecturers have both teaching and service obligations including curriculum development, pedagogical development and service to the Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills ( CPCS ) academic area, the Kelley School, Indiana University, and the broader community. Annual professional development funding is available for activities including conference travel, purchasing books, and research. These positions are based at the IUB campus in Bloomington, Indiana. Faculty members are expected to work on campus to fully engage with the area, school, campus, and community. About Kelley CPCS Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills ( CPCS ) is an integral part of the Kelley undergraduate curriculum. With more than 60 faculty, CPCS brings together instructors from a range of academic and professional backgrounds who teach courses in communication, career and professional development, and computer skills. All Kelley undergraduates complete courses offered by CPCS . CPCS faculty develop their own course materials while coordinating with colleagues teaching the same courses, providing both individual responsibility for the classroom and opportunities for curricular collaboration. Indiana University Bloomington, IU’s flagship campus, serves more than 47,000 students in a vibrant college town, while Kelley undergraduates bring strong academic and professional engagement from across the U.S. and around the world. Application Information Interested applicants should review the position requirements and apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34552 . Questions may be directed to Rachel Landis, Search and Selection Committee Chair, landisr@iu.edu. To ensure consideration, applications should be submitted by January 1, 2027. The search committee will continue reviewing applications until the positions are filled. References will be requested later in the process. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications Candidates must hold a Ph.D. (or ABD with completion by August 2027), master’s degree, or other postgraduate degree in Communication, Business, Journalism, Law, English, Education, Human Resources, Public Relations, Humanities, or a related field. Experience or expertise in professional or workplace communication is especially valuable. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise or experience in the following areas: ·         business, professional, or workplace communication; ·         audience analysis and rhetorical decision-making across written and oral professional genres; ·         business writing and/or business presentations; ·         team-based communication and collaborative work; ·         client-based, experiential, or project-based instruction; ·         instructional methods and curricular development; ·         teaching students from varied backgrounds and experiences; ·         the implications of generative AI for human-centered professional communication; and ·         collaborative faculty work, including curriculum initiatives, program assessment, and service. Department Contact for Questions Questions may be directed to Rachel Landis, Search and Selection Committee Chair, landisr@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/01/2027 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101715-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website * Would you prefer to teach business presentations, business writing or both? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34575": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical positions, Kelley School of Business (Operations and Decision Technologies, Information Systems)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank Clinical positions, Kelley School of Business (Operations and Decision Technologies, Information Systems) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank Clinical positions, Kelley School of Business (Operations and Decision Technologies, Information Systems) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To ensure consideration, applications should be received by September 20, 2026; however, the search will continue until the positions are filled. Interested applicants should review position requirements and apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34084 . The optional application document listed as “Other” should include Sample Course Material.  Sample Course Materials can include teaching materials, cases developed, assignments etc. Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank Clinical positions, Kelley School of Business (Operations and Decision Technologies, Information Systems) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Operations and Decision Technologies Location Bloomington Position Summary The Operations and Decision Technologies ( ODT ) Department of the Kelley School of Business at Indiana University – Bloomington invites applications for an open-rank, non-tenure-track clinical faculty position in Information Systems (IS) beginning in Fall 2027. The ODT Department consists of the Information Systems, Operations Management, and Decision Sciences groups and offers IS programs at the Undergraduate, Master’s, and Doctoral levels. The Kelley undergraduate program in Information Systems is ranked #6 , and our graduate programs in IS are ranked #8 by US News & World Report. Clinical faculty play a critical role in the dept and School’s success. We offer opportunities to advance through the ranks, and clinical faculty are eligible for leadership roles within the School and the University. Basic Qualifications For a candidate to be considered at the Clinical classification, a PhD, DBA , or ABD in information systems, computer science, information technology, or a closely related field is required. The degree should be completed by August 2027. Candidates should have professional experience, and ideally, experience teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The major teaching responsibilities involve information systems courses (including enterprise systems, computer programming, database, AI in Business, Business Analytics, Machine Learning/Deep Learning/Reinforcement Learning, Advanced topics in AI, and other technical courses) in the undergraduate and master’s programs. Research in IS pedagogy is also desirable. Department Contact for Questions Candidates should direct any questions to Claire Tafoya, odt@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions To ensure consideration, applications should be received by September 20, 2026; however, the search will continue until the positions are filled. Interested applicants should review position requirements and apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34084 . The optional application document listed as “Other” should include Sample Course Material.  Sample Course Materials can include teaching materials, cases developed, assignments etc. For Best Consideration Date 09/20/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101640-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Statement of Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents Other Sample Syllabus Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34573": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Human Biology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Lecturer in Human Biology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Lecturer in Human Biology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Lecturer in Human Biology Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Human Biology Program Location Bloomington Position Summary The Human Biology Program in the College of Arts and Sciences https://humanbio.indiana.edu at Indiana University invites applications for a permanent full-time 10-month non-tenure-track position at the level of Lecturer. We seek applicants with expertise in microbiomes, evolutionary medicine and comparative approaches, human-wildlife interactions and One Health, and/or environmental health and health disparities and a record of excellence in teaching at the undergraduate level. Although non-tenure track, the position has a promotion track from Lecturer to Senior Lecturer to Teaching Professor. Teaching responsibilities will be in the Human Biology Program with three courses taught per semester. Some of these courses will assist with growing experiential learning opportunities, including internships, undergraduate research, and establishing a new lab classroom, while others will cover our B200, B300, and/or B400 core courses. Applicants should apply online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34517 The IU Human Biology Program is a rapidly growing academic unit with almost 1000 students that offers BS, BA, and minor degrees. It is one of the largest and fastest growing majors on campus. Much of this success is due to IU Human Biology’s innovative educational practices, including interdisciplinary team teaching and active student research, that promote a holistic approach to scientific issues as they relate to the biology of humans. Current faculty within the program share appointments with numerous academic departments across the College of Arts and Sciences, including Anthropology, Biology, Geography, History and Philosophy of Science and Medicine, and Psychological and Brain Sciences. We encourage applicants from these disciplines and adjacent fields to apply. Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your educational background, research and teaching experience and current interests, and reasons for interest in the position; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of teaching philosophy including evidence of teaching ability, and (4) the names and contact information for three letters of recommendation. Questions about the position should be addressed to the Human Biology Program ( HUBIadm@iu.edu ) and the IU Human Biology Program Director, Dr. Michael Wasserman (mdwasser@iu.edu). Review of applications will begin Oct. 19. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications A Ph.D. in Biological Anthropology, Biology, or a related Natural Science field is required. Area of expertise should have clear relevance to human biology and this should be communicated clearly in the cover letter. Candidates should have strong communication and organizational skills and documented evidence of classroom and/or laboratory teaching excellence. Salary is commensurate with experience and full benefits are included. The expected start date is August 1, 2027. Although a permanent position, the initial appointment is for three years. Department Contact for Questions Questions about the position should be addressed to the Human Biology Program ( HUBIadm@iu.edu ) and the IU Human Biology Program Director, Dr. Michael Wasserman (mdwasser@iu.edu). Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/19/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101711-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34562": {
      "title": "Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Professional Skills)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Professional Skills) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Professional Skills) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank Lecturer positions, Kelley School of Business (Professional Skills) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills Location Bloomington Position Summary The Kelley School of Business at Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for full-time, open-rank Lecturer positions in Professional Skills beginning Fall 2027. These are 10-month, non-tenure-track appointments with an 18-credit-hour annual teaching assignment. Professional Skills faculty teach primarily in Kelley Compass, a required three-course professional-development sequence for Kelley undergraduates. The sequence integrates career development with professional communication, leadership, teamwork, and professional identity development. Faculty typically have a primary assignment in one of the three Compass courses, with opportunities to teach across the sequence. Professional Skills Courses Compass 1: Professional Identity and Career Exploration (1.5 credits) Compass 1 introduces students to career development and professional identity. Students explore their values, interests, personality, and skills; investigate majors and career possibilities; learn to articulate their professional identity and value to professional audiences; and develop professional networking skills. Compass 2: Career Readiness and Job Search (1.5 credits) Compass 2 develops students’ career-readiness and recruiting skills. Course content includes employer research and career strategy, resumes, interviewing, professional correspondence and networking, giving and receiving actionable feedback, cultural competence, and emotional intelligence. Compass 3: Professional Communication, Leadership, and Teaming (1 credit) Compass 3 is the capstone of the required professional development sequence (this is an online, asynchronous course). The course emphasizes evidence-based professional communication, leadership, emotional intelligence, and effective participation in high-performance virtual teams. The Faculty Role Lecturers have both teaching and service obligations including curriculum development, pedagogical development and service to the Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills ( CPCS ) academic area, the Kelley School, Indiana University, and the broader community. Annual professional development funding is available for activities including conference travel, purchasing books, and research. These positions are based at the IUB campus in Bloomington, Indiana. Faculty members are expected to work on campus to fully engage with the area, school, campus, and community. About Kelley CPCS Communication, Professional, and Computer Skills ( CPCS ) is an integral part of the Kelley undergraduate curriculum. With more than 60 faculty, CPCS brings together instructors from a range of academic and professional backgrounds who teach courses in communication, career and professional development, and computer skills. All Kelley undergraduates complete courses offered by CPCS . CPCS faculty develop their own course materials while coordinating with colleagues teaching the same courses, providing both individual responsibility for the classroom and opportunities for curricular collaboration. Indiana University Bloomington, IU’s flagship campus, serves more than 47,000 students in a vibrant college town, while Kelley undergraduates bring strong academic and professional engagement from across the U.S. and around the world. Application Information Interested applicants should review the position requirements and apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34546 . Questions may be directed to Rachel Landis, Search and Selection Committee Chair, landisr@iu.edu. To ensure consideration, applications should be submitted by January 1, 2027. The search committee will continue reviewing applications until the positions are filled. References will be requested later in the process. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications Candidates must hold a Ph.D. (or ABD with completion by August 2027), master’s degree, or other postgraduate degree in Career Development/Counseling, Organizational Development, Business, Communication, Human Resources, Management, Education, Psychology, or a related field. Experience or expertise in career development, corporate recruiting, or corporate training and development is also valued. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise or experience in the following areas: · career and self-development, including career exploration, career readiness, and professional identity; · recruiting and job-search practices, employer expectations, and evolving workplace trends; · teamwork, emotional intelligence, psychological safety, and leadership; · human-centered communication skills in response to evolving generative-AI technologies; · teaching students from varied backgrounds and experiences; · instructional methods and curricular development; · oral and written professional communication; and · collaborative faculty work, including curriculum initiatives, program assessment, and service. Department Contact for Questions Questions may be directed to Rachel Landis, Search and Selection Committee Chair, landisr@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/01/2027 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101713-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No * How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34557": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music (Double Bass); Tenure of Tenure-track position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music (Double Bass); Tenure of Tenure-track position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music (Double Bass); Tenure of Tenure-track position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. A complete application consists of a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and information about three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For those candidates who advance, an e-mail message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your reference providers. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration, however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music (Double Bass); Tenure of Tenure-track position Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Strings Location Bloomington Position Summary The Jacobs School of Music seeks candidates for a position in the Department of Strings in Double Bass. The successful candidate will have an extensive and successful background as a performer and teacher at the highest national and/or international level, will be able to work effectively with faculty colleagues, and will be able to inspire students in a professionally-oriented educational environment. Responsibilities: Teach double bass students in all degree and diploma programs, including freshmen through doctoral levels, teach studio class and possible teaching of orchestral repertoire class. Participation in departmental meetings, recruitment activities and auditions, doctoral committees, and recital hearing committees, along with other department activities, such as faculty recitals and outreach activities, master classes, service on Jacobs School of Music committees, and other similar activities is expected. Basic Qualifications Extensive performing and teaching experience at the very highest levels of the profession. Bachelor’s degree in music or equivalent training required, advanced degrees preferred; comparable professional experience will be also be considered. Department Contact for Questions Rachel Patrick, Office of the Executive Associate Dean: (812) 855-5541; rnpatric@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary and rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Special Instructions A complete application consists of a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and information about three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For those candidates who advance, an e-mail message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your reference providers. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration, however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. For Best Consideration Date 09/26/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101648-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34548": {
      "title": "Open Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Robotics",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Robotics Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Robotics Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Robotics Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Vice Provost For Faculty Affairs Location Bloomington Position Summary Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for a tenured/tenure-track assistant, associate, or full professor in the Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering, in the field of robotics. We seek exceptional researchers and educators whose work advances the science and engineering of intelligent, embodied systems and their societal applications. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to significantly contribute to the new Bachelor of Science in Robotics degree, shaping the next generation of roboticists through research-driven teaching and mentoring. We welcome applicants whose research advances human-centered robotics and human–robot interaction. We are particularly interested in scholarship in these areas addressing one or more of three interconnected themes: Interaction and collaboration: physical, haptic, and multimodal interaction; human–robot teaming; shared autonomy; and the design and study of interactive robotic systems. Embodied intelligence and mobility: robot design and control, humanoid robotics, whole-body control, and wearable robotic systems. Robotics in human environments: assistive, rehabilitation, healthcare, and field robotics, including the development, deployment, and study of robotic systems in real-world settings. We also welcome applicants whose work bridges these themes or brings complementary perspectives, methods, and application domains that broaden the scope of human-centered robotics at IUB . We encourage applications from scholars who apply interdisciplinary perspectives and share important research overlap with current IUB scholars while also offering new dimensions that will expand and strengthen IUB’s robotics research program . This 10‐month appointment would ideally start August 1, 2027 , although an earlier or later start date is negotiable. Applications received by November 15, 2026 , will receive full consideration, and applications will be accepted until the positions are filled. Responsibilities: Tenured/tenure-track faculty at Indiana University Bloomington are expected to substantially contribute to the overall academic excellence of their department by developing and/or maintaining a robust research portfolio including pursuing and obtaining major extramural research funding to conduct cutting-edge research both as a corresponding principal investigator and a collaborating investigator; teaching and mentoring undergraduate and/or graduate students as designated by the department chairperson; and actively providing service within the department, school and university alongside active engagement with the profession and/or the community. This position is located on the campus in Bloomington, Indiana, and a successful candidate is expected to work on the IUB campus to allow full engagement with the department, school, campus, and community. Indiana University bases its tenure and promotion recommendations upon performance in three areas including research, teaching, and service. All individuals hired through this initiative are expected to excel in research and provide strong performance in teaching and service. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a PhD in human-robot interaction, robotics, computer science, engineering, cognitive science (or a related field) and be committed to excellence in scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Candidates for a position with tenure must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment w/tenure, including extramurally funded research as a corresponding principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, established record of publications and scholarly work, and experience leading sizeable research initiatives. Application Procedures: Review of applications will begin November 16, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Application materials should include: Cover letter (maximum of 3 pages in length, identifying your application for a position with tenure (associate professor, professor) or a tenure-track position (assistant professor), summarizing your qualifications, and interest in the position. Current Curriculum Vitae Research Statement (maximum of 4 pages in length) Teaching Statement (maximum of 2 pages in length) Up to three representative scholarly publications (optional) Contact information for three professional references Professional references for applicants for tenure-track positions will only be solicited for those selected for a screening interviews. Professional references for applicants for positions with tenure will only be solicited for those selected for a finalist interview and/or job offer. All application materials should be submitted at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34541 All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Dr. Lantao Liu, search chairperson, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by IUB with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by IUB on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that IUB takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. About Indiana University Bloomington : Founded in 1820, Indiana University-Bloomington ( IUB ) is the flagship campus of Indiana University. Located in Bloomington, IN., IUB constantly strives to embody its mission to create, disseminate, preserve, and apply knowledge. It does so through its commitments to cutting-edge research, scholarship, arts, and creative activity; inspired and challenging undergraduate, graduate, professional, and lifelong educational programs; meaningful experiences outside the classroom; expansive global educational opportunities; first-rate library and museum collections; and far-reaching economic development and impact in the state and region. IUB is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities ( AAU ) since 1909, is accredited by the Higher Learning Commission and is a member of the North Central Association. With over 47,000 undergraduate and graduate students, the campus has 16 degree-granting colleges and schools offering 300+ undergraduate majors, 300+ Master’s degrees, and 150+ doctoral and professional degree opportunities. Known for its Indiana limestone facades and green spaces throughout, IUB is widely considered one of the country’s most beautiful college campuses. The Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering at Indiana University is the first of its kind and among the largest in the country. Its mission is to excel and lead in education, research, and outreach spanning and integrating the full breadth of computing, information technology and modern engineering. The Luddy School is a core school with programs on the Bloomington and Indianapolis campus and includes over 150 faculty and 3000 students. Departments in the Luddy School’s Bloomington campus include Computer Science, Information and Library Science, Informatics, and Intelligent Systems Engineering. Successful candidates will join a vibrant and interdisciplinary robotics community that spans informatics, computer science, intelligent systems engineering, cognitive science, and more. The Luddy School hosts state-of-the-art robotics labs and is home to robust initiatives in AI, autonomy, and human-centered computing. Faculty will have the opportunity to contribute to the implementation of a new Bachelor of Science in Robotics degree, shaping the next generation of roboticists through research-driven teaching and mentoring. Nestled in the rolling hills of south-central Indiana, and home to 85,000 residents, the city of Bloomington offers the feel of a college town with many of the amenities of a large city. From off-Broadway plays and musicals, the LOTUS World Festival of Music, quick access to 250,000 acres of public recreation lands, outstanding public schools and a reasonable cost of living, Bloomington offers much to all. For more information about the Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering, and Indiana University Bloomington, visit: Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering: https://luddy.indiana.edu/ IU Bloomington: https://bloomington.iu.edu/ Basic Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a PhD in human-robot interaction, robotics, computer science, engineering, cognitive science (or a related field) and be committed to excellence in scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Candidates for a position with tenure must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment w/tenure, including extramurally funded research as a corresponding principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, established record of publications and scholarly work, and experience leading sizeable research initiatives. Department Contact for Questions All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Dr. Lantao Liu, search chairperson, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/16/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101712-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy Research Agenda Optional Documents Publication 1 Publication 2 Publication 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34547": {
      "title": "Jewish Studies Program&#39;s Olamot Center Visiting Fellow at Indiana University-Bloomington for 2027-28 Academic Year",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Jewish Studies Program&#39;s Olamot Center Visiting Fellow at Indiana University-Bloomington for 2027-28 Academic Year Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Jewish Studies Program's Olamot Center Visiting Fellow at Indiana University-Bloomington for 2027-28 Academic Year Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Jewish Studies Program's Olamot Center Visiting Fellow at Indiana University-Bloomington for 2027-28 Academic Year Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Jewish Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary Jewish Studies Program’s Olamot Center Visiting Fellow Indiana University-Bloomington for 2027-28 Academic Year The Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program at Indiana University’s Center for Scholarly and Cultural Exchange with Israel, the Olamot Center, welcomes applications for a Visiting Lecturer position and Melanie and Andrew Goodman Postdoctoral Fellow for the academic year 2026-27 with the possibility of renewal for a second year, contingent on successful performance and available funding. Founded in 2017, the Olamot Center aims to realize the substantial potential for scholarly dialogue and collaboration with scholars in Israeli institutions of higher learning by offering Israeli scholars an opportunity to teach and conduct research in one of the largest and oldest Jewish Studies Programs in North America. The Olamot Center Visiting Lecturer and Postdoctoral Fellow will teach three undergraduate courses per year (one in Fall 2027; two in Spring 2028) in the history, society, and culture of modern Israel, complementing the offerings of the Borns Jewish Studies Program (3 courses total). The Fellow will also present at a workshop for the Program on their main area of research. The Fellow is expected to participate in the activities of the Borns JSP and to contribute to intellectual life at IU. Ph.D. is required ( ABD candidates will be considered). Area of expertise within Jewish Studies is open. The salary for one academic year is $57,000 plus benefits. The Fellow is responsible for arranging their own travel, housing accommodation, and living expenses during their term of appointment, August 2027 through May 2028, and must be able to fulfill their teaching responsibilities on the Indiana University Bloomington campus while classes are in session. To gain final administrative approval, the successful candidate is required to provide verification of credentials and other information required by law, including federally required documentation confirming citizenship or permanent residency of the United States or an authorized alien entitled to be employed in the U.S. for the period of appointment. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their application electronically at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/32771 . Application materials include a cover letter, C.V., short writing sample (20-30 pages), contact information for three references, and a list of three suggested courses. Questions about the application process or the application can be directed to: Olamot Visiting Scholar Position, Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University, Hamiliton Lugar School of Global and International Studies, 355 N. Eagleson Avenue, Room 4004A, Bloomington, IN 47405-1105, or via email to iujsp@iu.edu with the subject line “Olamot Visiting Fellow Position”. Consideration of applications will begin on November 16, 2026 and will remain open until filled, with priority given to applications submitted before November 16, 2026. More information about the Olamot Center can be found on its website at https://olamot.indiana.edu/. Questions about the position can be sent to Professor Irit Dekel, the Director of the Center, at idekel@iu.edu. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . Basic Qualifications Ph.D. is required ( ABD candidates will be considered). Area of expertise within Jewish Studies is open. Department Contact for Questions Questions about the application process or the application can be directed to: Olamot Visiting Scholar Position, Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University, Hamiliton Lugar School of Global and International Studies, 355 N. Eagleson Avenue, Room 4004A, Bloomington, IN 47405-1105, or via email to iujsp@iu.edu with the subject line “Olamot Visiting Fellow Position”. More information about the Olamot Center can be found on its website at https://olamot.indiana.edu/. Questions about the position can be sent to Professor Irit Dekel, the Director of the Center, at idekel@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank $57,000 - Visiting Faculty/Scholar (FVX) ; RSP / FV5 Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/16/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101541-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample List Of References Proposed Course Description Optional Documents Other Other 2 Other 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34539": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher - SOCIAL",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Researcher - SOCIAL Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Researcher - SOCIAL Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. However, we will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and apply online at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34399 We will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Application Materials: 1) CV 2) Brief Statement of Research Interests 3) Contact information for at least 2 references Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Researcher - SOCIAL Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Psychological & Brain Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary Postdoctoral Researcher – Study of Affective Disorders’ Classification and Treatment ( SADCAT ) Lab Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences at Indiana University Bloomington The SADCAT Lab (PI: Lorenzo Lorenzo-Luaces) at Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) is an interdisciplinary research lab dedicated to advancing our understanding of internalizing disorders —particularly depression and anxiety—and improving their treatment through data-driven, empirically grounded approaches. We are interested in the intersection of technology, mental health, and data science in clinical psychology. The lab is currently supported by an R01 Research Project Grant from the National Institutes of Health ( NIH ; PIs Lorenzo-Luaces & Bollen), investigating the impact of social media on adolescent mental health. This project aims to clarify the bidirectional relationship between social media use and internalizing symptoms in a longitudinal sample of 1,000 adolescents. Essential Duties The postdoctoral researcher will contribute to ongoing projects examining internalizing disorders, technology, cognitive-behavioral therapies (CBTs), and the personalization of treatment. The main project focuses on adolescents (ages 13–20) and employs a longitudinal design, passive data collections, and ecological momentary assessments ( EMA ) to capture the dynamic nature of symptoms. The position involves engaging with multi-level datasets and contributing to statistical analyses. The researcher may also contribute to manuscript preparation and help develop new studies that extend the lab’s mission of using data science to advance clinical psychology and improve mental health outcomes. The anticipated start date is Fall 2027. Start date is negotiable. Salary for this position is based on the NIH postdoctoral salary guidelines, with a starting salary of $$63,480.00, commensurate with experience. Basic Qualifications Required Education: Ideal candidates will have either of these degrees, both are not required. Ph.D. in clinical psychology or a related field (e.g., developmental) OR Ph.D. in informatics or a related discipline (e.g., computer science) Required Experience: Experience recruiting and retaining adolescents in longitudinal research Experience analyzing intensive longitudinal data (e.g. EMA ) Department Contact for Questions Questions may be sent to: Dr. Lorenzo Lorenzo-Luaces (lolorenz@iu.edu) Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. However, we will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and apply online at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34399 We will continue accepting applications until the position is filled. Application Materials: 1) CV 2) Brief Statement of Research Interests 3) Contact information for at least 2 references For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101710-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34538": {
      "title": "Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Positions, Kelley School of Business (Accounting)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Positions, Kelley School of Business (Accounting) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Positions, Kelley School of Business (Accounting) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33905 . We will not begin to consider applications until September 14, 2026, so candidates wishing full consideration should apply by that date; however, we will continue to consider applications from candidates who apply after that date until we fill the positions. Posting Details Position Details Title Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Positions, Kelley School of Business (Accounting) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Accounting Location Bloomington Position Summary The Accounting Department at the Kelley School of Business, Indiana University-Bloomington invites applications for open rank non-tenure-track faculty positions to begin fall semester 2027, with a start date of August 1, 2027. These positions are based at the IUB campus in Bloomington, Indiana. Faculty members are expected to work on campus to fully engage with the department, school, campus, and community. Basic Qualifications For a candidate to be considered at the Clinical classification, a Ph.D. degree in Accounting or in a related field, from a nationally reputed university must be completed by no later than August 2027. Candidates to be considered at the Lecturer rank must have a master’s degree in Accounting or in a related field. We encourage applications from candidates who have significant professional experience in accounting, an accounting certification ( CPA or CMA ), and a record of high-quality teaching and service in accounting. Department Contact for Questions Candidates should direct any questions to Bree Josefy, Associate Chair of the Department of Accounting ( bjosefy@iu.edu ). Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their application at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33905 . We will not begin to consider applications until September 14, 2026, so candidates wishing full consideration should apply by that date; however, we will continue to consider applications from candidates who apply after that date until we fill the positions. For Best Consideration Date 09/14/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101618-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
