    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34537": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Landeta Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Landeta Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Landeta Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Landeta Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The department of Biology is a large, unified department with strong undergraduate degrees, nationally-ranked graduate programs, and world-class research spanning the breadth of biological questions and experimental systems – from ecosystems to microbiology and developmental biology, from evolution to cell biology, from molecular biology to systems biology, bioinformatics, and genomics. It is always an exciting time for Biology – enormous advances in global genome analysis coupled with unprecedented developments in interdisciplinary research have made the 21st century the Century of Biology. For more information about the department, you can find it here: About: Department of Biology: Indiana University Bloomington. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Landeta Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University, Bloomington is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow. Our department is a large, unified department with world-class research spanning all levels of biological organization and diverse experimental systems. Our lab in Microbiology investigates the processes that take place in the bacterial cell envelope with emphasis into oxidative protein folding. The overarching goal for this position is to make timely progress on our NIH and CFF grants ( https://reporter.nih.gov/search/kpoQKsh8Pkqp4S1_RS1-PQ/project-details/11259946 ), which looks for fundamental insights into novel oxidative reactions, the exported proteins that depend on disulfide bonds, the substrate specificity among thiol-disulfide oxidoreductases and finding novel inhibitors against oxidative folding. We are excited about science and looking for another positive, curious person to join our team. Core topics to work on: (1) In vitro assays to determine inhibitor efficacy against redox membrane proteins (2) Cysteine enrichment of DsbA substrates in E. coli and Mycobacteria using proteomics. (3) determine differences between DsbA proteins of two model organisms, E. coli and M. smegmatis (4) Establishing termini restraining for inhibitor-protein crystallography in collaboration with Prof. Weikai Li. Other general responsibilities include: Supervising undergrads; managing general lab operations; writing up research (e.g. for our annual reports or for manuscripts); presenting findings at lab meetings, microphiles, retreats and conferences; and participating in lab meetings. Basic Qualifications Education and Experience Required: PhD Qualifications: Highly motivated candidates with a Ph.D., in microbiology, biochemistry, biology, or a related field, with a strong interest in studying oxidative protein folding in bacteria and targeting this process in pathogens. High degree of initiative, time management and professionalism, very organized and detail-oriented with proficient communication skills, written and oral and ability to work effectively as a team. Experience with microbial genetics, protein purification, in vitro assays, molecular biology, proteomics is desirable. Trainees will receive health insurance as well as a stipend commensurate with experience. To apply, please submit (1) a cover letter describing your research and educational background, your career goals, why you are interested in this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV highlighting your publications including a brief description of the main findings and how you contributed to the work, and (3) the names and contact info of three references. This can be submitted at the following link: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33897 . Department Contact for Questions For questions about the position, contact Cristina (clandeta@iu.edu). Timing and Compensation: Review of applicants will begin asap and continue until the position is filled. Ideal start date is Oct 1, 2026. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment for up to 3 years is contingent upon satisfactory performance. Salary will be commensurate with experience, and full benefits are included. The best consideration date is 09/22. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/22/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101616-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34535": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-track Faculty Position in Mathematical Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Tenured/Tenure-track Faculty Position in Mathematical Artificial Intelligence Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Tenured/Tenure-track Faculty Position in Mathematical Artificial Intelligence Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Tenured/Tenure-track Faculty Position in Mathematical Artificial Intelligence Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Vice Provost For Faculty Affairs Location Bloomington Position Summary As part of Indiana University Bloomington’s Faculty 100 initiative, the Department of Mathematics in the College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for an open-rank position (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) to begin in Fall 2027. These are 10-month appointments, ideally beginning August 1, 2027. We seek an outstanding mathematician whose research engages substantially with artificial intelligence, data science, or scientific machine learning. The successful candidate will have a primary appointment in Mathematics and opportunities to collaborate broadly with faculty in the Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering, Data Science, Statistics, the Institute for Scientific Computing and Applied Mathematics, and scientific units across our campus. We are particularly interested in researchers working in mathematical artificial intelligence, broadly construed. This includes, but is not limited to: machine learning algorithms, formal proof assistants, and large language models. Areas of interest for possible collaborations include but are not limited to: topological data analysis and topological machine learning; AI-assisted theorem proving, formal verification, proof assistants, automated proof search, and AI-guided mathematical discovery; neural operators, physics-informed learning, inverse problems, uncertainty quantification, and rigorous methods for differential equations and scientific machine learning; geometric deep learning, manifold learning, equivariant methods, high-dimensional geometry, and structure-aware learning; and the mathematical foundations of trustworthy AI, including stability, robustness, verification, interpretability, and generalization. Candidates whose work connects rigorous mathematics with modern computation and complements departmental strengths in topology, algebraic topology, analysis, partial differential equations, geometric analysis, probability, logic, formal methods, and experimental mathematics will be given preference. We encourage applications from scholars who apply interdisciplinary perspectives across a variety of these domains. Responsibilities: Candidates are expected to sustain an active, externally funded research program, to engage undergraduate and graduate students through effective teaching and mentoring, to contribute to curricular development in mathematical AI as described above, and to participate in service to the university and the profession. Candidates should be able to work effectively in a team environment and to build connections between mathematics, the joint B.S. program with Luddy Data Science, and AI- and data-intensive research across campus. To advance scholarly innovation and excellence, we especially welcome candidates whose demonstrated experience, research, teaching, and mentoring will support the university’s commitment to an inclusive academic community. Application Procedures: All applicants should apply online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34236 and also at https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28843 through which all materials are requested. Materials required include: a cover letter of application stating rank sought; CV; and statements on research and teaching. Also required are three letters of reference. Applicants seeking appointment with tenure should provide an additional three names of potential references from which the department may later solicit additional letters. Full consideration date. For full consideration, applications should be received by November 7, 2026 . Review of applications will begin immediately thereafter. Before any conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. About Indiana University Bloomington: Founded in 1820, Indiana University-Bloomington ( IUB ) is the flagship campus of Indiana University. Located in Bloomington, IN., IUB constantly strives to embody its mission to create, disseminate, preserve, and apply knowledge. It does so through its commitments to cutting-edge research, scholarship, arts, and creative activity; inspired and challenging undergraduate, graduate, professional, and lifelong educational programs; meaningful experiences outside the classroom; expansive global educational opportunities; first-rate library and museum collections; and far-reaching economic development and impact in the state and region. IUB is a member of the prestigious Association of American University ( AAU ) since 1909, is accredited by the Higher Learning Commission and is a member of the North Central Association. With over 47,000 undergraduate and graduate students, the campus has 16 degree-granting colleges and schools offering 300+ undergraduate majors, 300+ Master’s degrees, and 150+ doctoral and professional degree opportunities. Known for its Indiana limestone facades and green spaces throughout, IUB is widely considered one of the country’s most beautiful college campuses. The College of Arts and Sciences at Indiana University offers over 225 academic programs rooted in three broad areas of study—Arts and Humanities, Natural and Mathematics Sciences, and Social and Historical Studies. The College is home to over 70 departments and programs and three schools—the Eskenazi School of Art, Architecture + Design, the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies, and The Media School. Over 900 faculty members serve within the College and form an extraordinary teaching and learning community at the heart of a world-class university. Members of the College’s faculty are continually recognized as elected fellows of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, the National Academies, the American Association for the Advancement of Science, and include MacArthur and Guggenheim Fellows, Fulbright Award winners, and Pulitzer Prize, Oscar, and Emmy winners. The Department of Mathematics at Indiana University Bloomington supports research and teaching across pure and applied mathematics. Its strengths in topology, algebraic topology, analysis, partial differential equations, geometric analysis, logic, formal methods, and experimental mathematics provide a strong foundation for this position. The successful candidate will help connect these strengths to a developing course in topological data analysis, the new joint B.S. major with Luddy Data Science, undergraduate research, graduate training, and campus collaborations in AI, data science, and scientific computing. The Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering at Indiana University is the first of its kind and among the largest in the country. Its mission is to excel and lead in education, research, and outreach spanning and integrating the full breadth of computing, information technology and modern engineering. The Luddy School is a core school with programs on the Bloomington and Indianapolis campus and includes over 150 faculty and 3100 students. Departments in the Luddy School’s Bloomington campus include Computer Science, Information and Library Science, Informatics, and Intelligent Systems Engineering. Nestled in the rolling hills of south-central Indiana, and home to 85,000 residents, the city of Bloomington offers the feel of a college town with many of the amenities of a large city. From off-Broadway plays and musicals, the LOTUS World Festival of Music, quick access to 250,000 acres of public recreation lands, outstanding public schools and a reasonable cost of living, Bloomington offers much to all. Basic Qualifications Qualifications: Applicants should possess a PhD in Mathematics, Applied Mathematics, or a closely related field by the date of appointment and be committed to excellence in scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Appointment with tenure requires a record of research, teaching, and service appropriate for tenure at Indiana University Bloomington. Department Contact for Questions All queries about the position or the application procedures may be addressed to Chair Kevin Pilgrim pilgrim@iu.edu with cc to Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/07/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101659-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34534": {
      "title": "Open-Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Aging and Technology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Open-Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Aging and Technology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Open-Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Aging and Technology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Open-Rank, Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Aging and Technology Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Vice Provost For Faculty Affairs Location Bloomington Position Summary Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for an open-rank, tenured/tenure-track faculty position in the School of Public Health-Bloomington in the field of Aging and Technology. We seek candidates to join IU’s outstanding research enterprise in aging and gerontology and collaborate in areas such as social and behavioral science, digital health, behavioral epidemiology, bio-health informatics, human factors, robotics, implementation science, and neuroscience . The position is part of Indiana University’s Faculty 100 initiative, a strategic investment in exceptional scholars whose work advances the university’s research excellence and national impact. We encourage applications from scholars who bring interdisciplinary perspectives and whose research complements existing strengths at Indiana University, while introducing new directions that expand and strengthen the institution’s research capacity in aging and technology. This 10‐month appointment begins August 1, 2027 , although an earlier or later start date is negotiable. Applications received by October 16, 2026, will receive full consideration, and applications will be accepted until the positions are filled. Responsibilities Tenured/tenure-track faculty at Indiana University Bloomington are expected to contribute substantially to the university’s missions of research, teaching, and service. Responsibilities include: Developing and maintaining an externally funded and nationally recognized research program; Pursuing and securing major extramural funding as principal investigator and collaborative investigator; Publishing impactful scholarship in leading peer-reviewed outlets; Teaching and mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students; Engaging in interdisciplinary collaboration across Indiana University and with external partners; and Providing service and leadership within the department, school, university, profession, and community. Candidates with special interest in any of the following (or related) areas are encouraged to apply: Wearable & Ambient Sensors: Continuous physiological and behavioral monitoring; fall detection and prediction; dementia care and wandering prevention; activity tracking for chronic disease management. Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning: Predictive risk modeling for falls, hospitalization, and cognitive decline; Natural Language Processing ( NLP ) for remote cognitive assessment; image analysis for wound care; condition prediction and diagnosis. Conversational AI & Social Robotics: Companionship for socially isolated or lonely older adults; medication reminders for adherence; caregiver-facing information and guidance systems. Telehealth & Remote Monitoring: Chronic disease management; specialist access for rural populations; post-acute monitoring to prevent readmissions. Smart Home Technologies: Automated safety systems for dementia patients; environmental controls for mobility-limited adults; passive monitoring without wearables. Digital Biomarkers: Passively collected behavioral data from consumer devices enabling detection of cognitive decline, mood changes, and functional deterioration years before clinical presentation. Extended Reality (XR / VR): Pain management, cognitive rehabilitation, physical therapy engagement, and social connection for homebound and institutionalized older adults. This position is located on the Bloomington campus of Indiana University. Successful candidates are expected to work on campus to allow full engagement with the department, school, university, and community. Indiana University bases tenure and promotion recommendations upon performance in research, teaching, and service. Faculty hired through this initiative are expected to excel in research and demonstrate strong performance in teaching and service. The successful candidate will contribute to and help expand Indiana University’s nationally recognized aging research enterprise. The individual will have opportunities to collaborate broadly across Indiana University schools, centers, and institutes, including the Regenstrief Institute, the Irsay Institute, the Luddy School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering, the School of Medicine, the School of Nursing, and other units engaged in aging, health, technology, and data science research. The position is intended to catalyze innovative interdisciplinary partnerships, support multi-investigator research initiatives, and strengthen Indiana University’s competitiveness for large-scale federal, foundation, and industry-funded research programs. Minimum Qualifications Applicants must hold an earned doctoral degree in public health, behavioral science, social science, data science, informatics, engineering, neuroscience, rehabilitation science, health services research, or a closely related field by the date of appointment and demonstrate a commitment to excellence in research, teaching, mentoring, and service, appropriate to rank. Candidates for tenured positions must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment with tenure, including extramurally funded research as a principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, an established record of publications and scholarly contributions, and experience leading significant research initiatives. Application Procedures Review of applications will begin October 16, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Application materials should include: Cover letter (1 page maximum) Current Curriculum Vitae Research & Teaching Statement (maximum of 3 pages describing research experience and future goals, and an additional 2 pages on teaching and mentorship experience) Up to three representative scholarly publications (optional) Contact information for three professional references Professional references will only be solicited for candidates selected for a finalist interview and/or job offer. Applicants should apply at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34281 Questions regarding the position or application process may be directed to Dr. Matthew Lee Smith, Search Committee Chair ( matlsmit@iu.edu ), or Dr. Cindy Broderick, Search Coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. Candidates will also be required to authorize Indiana University Bloomington to inquire with current and former employers regarding such matters. The relevance of information disclosed or obtained during this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated on a case-by-case basis. Indiana University Bloomington takes matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. About the School of Public Health-Bloomington The Indiana University School of Public Health-Bloomington ( SPH -B) is one of the nation’s leading schools of public health and is committed to advancing health and well-being through excellence in research, teaching, service, and community engagement. Located on Indiana University’s flagship campus, SPH -B includes five academic departments and more than 300 faculty and staff dedicated to addressing complex public health challenges through innovative, interdisciplinary approaches. The School is home to nationally and internationally recognized scholars whose work spans public health, health behavior, epidemiology, environmental health, aging, health policy, physical activity, recreation, nutrition, and health technology. Faculty collaborate widely across Indiana University and with partners throughout the state, nation, and world to improve population health and well-being. The successful candidate will join the Department of Applied Health Science, a nationally recognized department committed to improving health and well-being through research, teaching, and community engagement. Faculty conduct interdisciplinary research across aging and gerontology, health behavior, chronic disease prevention and management, dementia and Alzheimer’s disease, sexuality and reproductive health, nutrition, physical activity, substance use, community health, implementation science, and health equity. The department has particular strengths in aging and gerontology, including research focused on dementia and Alzheimer’s disease, caregiving, chronic disease prevention and management, falls prevention, health behavior change, and technology-enabled approaches to improve health and quality of life among older adults. The School of Public Health-Bloomington is an ideal academic home for a scholar in Aging and Technology. Faculty within SPH -B are conducting innovative research in aging, chronic disease prevention and management, dementia and Alzheimer’s disease, caregiving, falls prevention, digital health, and technology-enabled approaches to improve health and quality of life among older adults. This position will strengthen and expand Indiana University’s substantial aging research ecosystem and multidisciplinary Aging Research Network. Indiana University provides faculty with exceptional support for teaching, scholarship, and research through programs such as the Faculty Academy on Excellence in Teaching (F.A.C.E.T.), extensive research development and administrative support through the Office of the Vice President for Research, and a rich culture of interdisciplinary collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to collaborate across Indiana University with leading scholars in public health, data science and artificial intelligence, bio-health informatics, neuroscience, nursing, medicine, pharmacy, human factors, rehabilitation sciences, environmental sciences, engineering, robotics, and social work. About Indiana University Bloomington Founded in 1820, Indiana University-Bloomington ( IUB ) is the flagship campus of Indiana University and a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities ( AAU ). With more than 47,000 students and 16 degree-granting schools and colleges, IU Bloomington offers an exceptional environment for academic excellence, innovation, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Located in south-central Indiana, Bloomington combines the resources of a world-class research university with the quality of life of a vibrant college town. The community offers a rich arts and cultural environment, outstanding outdoor recreation opportunities, excellent schools, and an affordable cost of living. For more information: School of Public Health-Bloomington: School of Public Health-Bloomington Indiana University Bloomington: Indiana University Bloomington Basic Qualifications Minimum Qualifications Applicants must hold an earned doctoral degree in public health, behavioral science, social science, data science, informatics, engineering, neuroscience, rehabilitation science, health services research, or a closely related field by the date of appointment and demonstrate a commitment to excellence in research, teaching, mentoring, and service, appropriate to rank. Candidates for tenured positions must demonstrate a record of scholarly achievement appropriate for an appointment with tenure, including extramurally funded research as a principal investigator, current funding or strong potential to secure external funding, an established record of publications and scholarly contributions, and experience leading significant research initiatives. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process may be directed to Dr. Matthew Lee Smith, Search Committee Chair ( matlsmit@iu.edu ), or Dr. Cindy Broderick, Search Coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Commensurate with experience Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/16/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101669-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy Statement of Research and Teaching Optional Documents Publication 1 Publication 2 Publication 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34533": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Virology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Virology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Virology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position search is being conducted concurrently with a tenured, associate professor position. To apply for the w/tenure position, visit: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34289 Posting Details Position Details Title Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Virology Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Vice Provost For Faculty Affairs Location Bloomington Position Summary Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor in the College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Biology in the field of virology . We seek candidates to join IU’s outstanding microbiology research program to lead research in the areas of cellular responses to virus infection, cell biology of virus infection, and evolution of emerging viruses, but other areas of virology will be considered. We encourage applications from scholars who apply interdisciplinary perspectives and share important research overlap with current IUB scholars, while also offering new dimensions that will expand and strengthen virology research at IUB . Researchers have access to state-of-the-art facilities for genomics and bioinformatics, light and electron microscopy, flow cytometry, proteomic analysis, physical biochemistry analytical instrumentation, and crystallography. This 10‐month appointment would ideally start August 1, 2027 , but start date is negotiable. Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the biology department at Indiana University Bloomington are expected to substantially contribute to the overall academic excellence of the department by developing and/or maintaining a robust research portfolio including pursuing and obtaining major extramural research funding to conduct cutting-edge research as a principal investigator, teaching undergraduate and graduate in the classroom, mentoring graduate students in our graduate programs, participating in service at the University and actively engaging with their professional community. Indiana University bases its tenure and promotion recommendations on performance in research, teaching, and service. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a doctoral degree and demonstrate a strong record of scholarly achievement appropriate to the level of appointment. Candidates applying at the assistant professor level should provide evidence of past and current research productivity through first- or corresponding-author peer-reviewed publications, presentations of research at relevant scientific or professional conferences, and other indicators of scholarly impact. Candidates should also demonstrate strong potential to secure external research funding. Application Procedures: Review of applications will begin October 16, 202 6 and continue until the position is filled. Application materials should include: Cover letter (maximum of 1 page in length summarizing your qualifications, and interest in the position). Current Curriculum Vitae Research Statement (maximum of 4 pages in length) describing past and future work Applicants for the assistant professor position should apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34288 and provide contact information for three letters of reference , which will be solicited at the time of application . All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Drs. Tuli Mukhopadhyay ( sumukhop@iu.edu ) and Pranav Danthi ( pdanthi@iu.edu ), search co-chairpersons, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. About the University and the Town: Founded in 1820, Indiana University Bloomington ( IUB ) is the flagship campus of Indiana University . A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities ( AAU ) since 1909, IUB is accredited by the Higher Learning Commission. The campus serves more than 47,000 undergraduate and graduate students across 16 degree-granting colleges and schools, offering more than 300 undergraduate majors, 300 master’s programs, and 150 doctoral and professional degree opportunities. IUB’s mission is to create, disseminate, preserve, and apply knowledge through cutting-edge research, scholarship, arts, and creative activity; challenging undergraduate, graduate, professional, and lifelong educational programs; meaningful experiences beyond the classroom; and expansive global educational opportunities. The University is also home to first-rate library and museum collections and contributes significantly to economic development and impact throughout Indiana and the region. Known for its Indiana limestone architecture and expansive green spaces, IUB is widely regarded as one of the country’s most beautiful college campuses. The College of Arts and Sciences is at the academic heart of Indiana University Bloomington. The College offers more than 225 academic programs spanning three broad areas: Arts and Humanities, Natural and Mathematical Sciences, and Social and Historical Studies. It is home to more than 70 departments and programs and three schools: the Eskenazi School of Art, Architecture + Design; the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies; and The Media School. More than 900 faculty members contribute to the College’s teaching, research, and creative mission. College faculty are regularly recognized with some of the nation’s and world’s highest academic and professional honors, including election to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, the National Academies, and the American Association for the Advancement of Science, as well as MacArthur and Guggenheim Fellowships, Fulbright Awards, Pulitzer Prizes, Oscars, and Emmys. The position is based in the Department of Biology , a large, unified department within the College of Arts and Sciences. Biology offers strong undergraduate programs, nationally ranked graduate programs, and a broad, collaborative research environment. Research in the department spans biological questions and experimental systems across scales, from ecosystems, evolution, and organismal biology to microbiology, developmental and cell biology, molecular and systems biology, bioinformatics, and genomics. This breadth provides opportunities for interdisciplinary research and collaboration across the department and the broader IUB research community. Located in the rolling hills of south-central Indiana, Bloomington is a vibrant community of approximately 85,000 residents that combines the character of a college town with many of the cultural and recreational amenities of a larger city. The community offers theater, music, and arts events—including the LOTUS World Music & Arts Festival—as well as quick access to approximately 250,000 acres of public recreation lands. With strong public schools and a reasonable cost of living, Bloomington offers an attractive environment in which to live and work. Basic Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Applicants should possess a doctoral degree and demonstrate a strong record of scholarly achievement appropriate to the level of appointment. Candidates applying at the assistant professor level should provide evidence of past and current research productivity through first- or corresponding-author peer-reviewed publications, presentations of research at relevant scientific or professional conferences, and other indicators of scholarly impact. Candidates should also demonstrate strong potential to secure external research funding. Department Contact for Questions All queries about the position or the application procedures should be addressed to Drs. Tuli Mukhopadhyay ( sumukhop@iu.edu ) and Pranav Danthi ( pdanthi@iu.edu ), search co-chairpersons, or Dr. Cindy Broderick, search coordinator, at faculty1@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions This position search is being conducted concurrently with a tenured, associate professor position. To apply for the w/tenure position, visit: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34289 For Best Consideration Date 10/16/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101671-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact:  At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact:  Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact:  Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement:  Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement:  IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement:  Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Research and Teaching Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
