    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34342": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Media, Behavior, and Health",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor in Media, Behavior, and Health Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor in Media, Behavior, and Health Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor in Media, Behavior, and Health Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Media School Location Bloomington Position Summary The Media School at Indiana University’s flagship Bloomington campus is pleased to announce a faculty search for an outstanding tenure-track colleague whose research and teaching examine the relationships among media, behavior, and health, to start August 1, 2027. We welcome applications from scholars working across a broad range of topics at the intersection of media, health, and well-being. Areas of interest may include, but are not limited to, media influences on mental and physical health; health communication and health behavior; social media and well-being; media representations of health, illness, and disability; stigma and stereotyping; cognitive, emotional, and behavioral responses to health-related media; health misinformation; and the development and evaluation of media-based interventions designed to improve health and well-being. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work helps explain how media environments, messages, and technologies shape health-related attitudes, psychological processes, decision-making, and behavioral outcomes. We welcome a range of theoretical perspectives and methodological approaches, including experimental, survey, longitudinal, computational, neuroscientific, qualitative, and mixed-method approaches. Candidates may study children, adolescents, adults, or populations across the lifespan. This position offers substantial opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across Indiana University, including with faculty in the Media School, the School of Public Health, the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, the Irsay Institute, and other units engaged in health and behavioral research. The successful candidate will join a growing community of scholars examining media, behavior, health, and well-being across the campus. The chosen applicant will be expected to maintain a productive and theoretically significant research program, effectively teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students on a standard 2-2 teaching load, and serve the university, profession, and wider community. Teaching opportunities may include courses in media psychology, strategic communications, health communication, social media, research methods, and other areas related to the candidate’s expertise. The primary criteria for this search are excellence in research and teaching related to media, behavior, and health. Evidence of potential to secure external funding is viewed favorably. External funding is conceived broadly to include government, industry, foundation, and other private sources. About the School The Media School combines a long-standing tradition of excellence in journalism, film, gaming, and communications with a commitment to digital and interactive media. It fosters a multidisciplinary approach to education and research, equipping students with the theoretical and practical tools necessary to lead and innovate in the media landscape of tomorrow. Faculty members are distinguished for their scholarly contributions and their dedication to teaching, offering a dynamic environment for engaging with media theory, production, and design. The Media School is home to the Center for Documentary Research and Practice, the Center for International Media Law and Policy Studies, the Institute for Communication Research, the Michael I. Arnolt Center for Investigative Journalism, the National Sports Journalism Center, the Observatory on Social Media, Indiana Public Media, Cream and Crimson Studios, the KIX Lab, and the Black Film Center & Archive. The school serves more than 2,300 undergraduate majors in eight degree programs (Journalism, Media, Game Design, Cinematic Arts, Global Media, Media Advertising, Public Relations, and Sports Media). It also offers graduate degrees (M.S. and Ph.D.) to approximately 75 graduate students, of whom two-thirds are in a doctoral program. Our state-of-the-art facilities include equipment for television, film and audio production, game design, and communication research. University and Community Setting Nestled in the lively community of Bloomington, our university campus is renowned for its stunning limestone architecture and scenic natural landscapes. This setting fosters a culture that values both the arts and sciences and provides substantial opportunities for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Bloomington is a vibrant midsize city located within driving distance of Indianapolis, Chicago, Cincinnati, Columbus, and Louisville. Our community is small yet welcoming, a home to people from all over the world. Bloomington features an active arts community, with several independent music and performance venues and one of the best university cinemas in the country. Southern Indiana is also home to beautiful scenery, and residents enjoy year-round outdoor activities. Basic Qualifications ABD applications considered, but Ph.D. in communication, media, psychology, public health, or a related field must be completed by the date of appointment. Demonstrated potential for research productivity in areas related to media, behavior, and health. Effective teaching skills adaptable to undergraduate and graduate learning environments. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding this position or the application process should be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Nicole Martins, The Media School, Indiana University, at nicomart@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated potential for interdisciplinary collaboration. Demonstrated potential to secure external funding. Demonstrated success working with faculty and student populations from a wide variety of backgrounds and experiences. Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/23/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101677-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Evidence of Teaching Ability Other Other 2 Other 3 Statement of Research and Teaching Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34317": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher – Quantum and Classical Dynamics of Decision Making",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Researcher – Quantum and Classical Dynamics of Decision Making Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Researcher – Quantum and Classical Dynamics of Decision Making Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application Instructions Interested individuals should apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34275 . A complete application includes: 1. Cover letter describing research interests and fit for the position 2. Curriculum vitae 3. A professional writing sample (e.g., publication or dissertation chapter) 4. Names and contact information for three references Applications received by the Best Consideration Date will receive full consideration; however, the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Researcher – Quantum and Classical Dynamics of Decision Making Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Psychological & Brain Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary Position Overview The Postdoctoral Researcher will join an NSF -funded project developing and testing computational models of how decisions evolve over time. The project integrates quantum and classical (Markov) approaches within an open-systems framework to investigate when decision dynamics exhibit interference, coherence, decoherence, and more classical stochastic accumulation. The position will be jointly mentored by Dr. Tim Pleskac and Dr. Jerome Busemeyer, with the postdoc taking a leading role in theory development, computational modeling, and experimental tests of the models. The position is based at Indiana University Bloomington, with opportunities to collaborate across IU’s strong communities in mathematical psychology, judgment and decision making, cognitive science, and quantitative modeling. This postdoctoral appointment is full-time and on-campus. Job Duties 80% – Research Develop computational models of dynamic judgment and decision making using quantum probability, open-system dynamics, and classical Markov processes. Derive and test behavioral predictions that distinguish quantum, classical, and intermediate open-system dynamics, including predictions about the time course of evidence and preference evolution. Design and analyze behavioral experiments that probe dynamic decision processes, including interference, oscillation, response-time, and evidence-accumulation phenomena. Implement simulation, parameter-estimation, and model-comparison methods in Python, MATLAB , R, or related computational environments. Lead and co-author manuscripts, conference presentations, and other dissemination of project findings. 10% – Project Management Work directly with the PIs to ensure efficient progress toward project objectives. Coordinate research tasks across graduate students, undergraduate research assistants, and collaborating researchers. Maintain reproducible modeling, simulation, data-analysis, and documentation workflows. 10% – Other Duties Participate in lab meetings, mentoring of junior researchers, grant-related activities, and other project duties as needed. Work Environment The postdoc will be embedded in the rich cognitive and mathematical psychology research communities at Indiana University. The researcher will work closely with Drs. Pleskac and Busemeyer and benefit from Indiana University’s long-standing strengths in mathematical psychology, decision science, and quantum cognition. Appointment Details Anticipated Start Date: January 1, 2027 (negotiable) Salary: $60,000 + benefits Work Schedule: Full-time, on-campus Supervisors: Dr. Tim Pleskac and Dr. Jerome Busemeyer Initial Appointment: 1 year, with expectation of renewal for a second year based on performance and funding Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications Ph.D. preferably in Psychology or Cognitive Science, but we will also consider strong candidates in Computer Science, Physics, or a related quantitative fields by the start date. Demonstrated expertise in mathematical or computational modeling and dynamical systems. Proficiency in either Python, MATLAB , R, or a comparable scientific programming environment. Strong quantitative, analytic, and scientific writing skills. Department Contact for Questions Questions may be sent to: Dr. Tim Pleskac (tpleskac@iu.edu) Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications Experience or strong interest in quantum cognition. Background in stochastic processes, Markov models, dynamical systems, quantum walks, or related mathematical approaches. Experience with computational model fitting, Bayesian inference, simulation, or formal model comparison. Experience designing or analyzing experiments in judgment and decision making, evidence accumulation, or response-time dynamics. Ability to move between mathematical theory, computational implementation, and empirical testing. Interest in interdisciplinary work at the intersection of cognitive science, mathematical psychology, decision theory, and quantum-inspired modeling. Salary and Rank Special Instructions Application Instructions Interested individuals should apply at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34275 . A complete application includes: 1. Cover letter describing research interests and fit for the position 2. Curriculum vitae 3. A professional writing sample (e.g., publication or dissertation chapter) 4. Names and contact information for three references Applications received by the Best Consideration Date will receive full consideration; however, the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. For Best Consideration Date 10/12/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101666-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Research Paper/Publication/Writing Sample List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
