    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34265": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Global Security Studies",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant Professor of Global Security Studies Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant Professor of Global Security Studies Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant Professor of Global Security Studies Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington International Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of International Studies at Indiana University’s Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies invites applications for an assistant professor, tenure-track faculty position in Security Studies beginning August 1, 2027. We seek a scholar whose research and teaching focus on security studies. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, grand strategy, the causes of war, nonproliferation, and how new technologies may affect international security and geopolitics. Scholars with regional expertise within security studies are welcome to apply, as well. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s strengths in international and global studies and a growing focus on security studies across IU-Bloomington. The candidate’s teaching will support existing undergraduate and graduate programs, including the growing Master of International Affairs ( MIA ) program. We welcome applications from candidates employing diverse disciplinary and methodological approaches. Candidates should demonstrate a strong research agenda, evidence of or potential for excellence in teaching, and a commitment to interdisciplinary engagement within an international studies environment. Responsibilities include maintaining an active research program, teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in areas related to global security studies, mentoring students, and contributing to departmental and university service. Applicants must have a doctoral degree in hand by August 1, 2027. Review of applications will begin following a priority deadline of October 16, 2026, with all applications received by that date being guaranteed full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, a writing sample, and the contact information for three references. Basic Qualifications Candidates should: hold a PhD in International Studies, Political Science, Political Economy, History, Law, Geography, Sociology, or a related field at the time of appointment; possess a record of scholarly achievement commensurate with rank, and; demonstrate evidence of teaching excellence or strong potential for effective teaching. Department Contact for Questions Inquiries regarding the position, the Department of International Studies, and the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies may be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Hussein Banai ( hbanai@iu.edu ). Questions regarding technical aspects of the application process may be directed to Mr. Zac Muller, Hamilton Lugar School Assistant Director of Faculty Relations, at zmuller@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information . For Best Consideration Date 10/16/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101661-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy Research Agenda Writing Sample Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34264": {
      "title": "Director of the Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program / Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Director of the Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program / Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Director of the Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program / Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Posting Details Position Details Title Director of the Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program / Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Jewish Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary The Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program at Indiana University, one of the world’s longest-established Jewish Studies programs, invites applications for its next Director. The appointment will be at the rank of full professor, beginning August 2027, and will be made in cooperation with the relevant disciplinary department. Candidates at the rank of associate professor would be considered in exceptional cases. Depending on their research expertise, the Director may also be eligible to hold the Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies for the duration of their tenure. The Director will serve an initial four-year term, with the expectation of renewal for at least one additional term. As Director, the successful candidate will provide visionary leadership to sustain and grow a program that spans literature, history, politics, religious studies, gender studies, sociology, musicology, languages, and the arts. We seek a leader with the capacity to strengthen enrollment, expand partnerships, foster exchange of diverse viewpoints, and articulate and execute a compelling vision for the program’s scholarly profile, public presence, and future growth. The program is home to two centers of excellence: Olamot: Center for Scholarly and Cultural Exchange with Israel, which fosters academic and cultural exchange with Israeli partners, and the Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism ( ISCA ), which advances the study of and teaching about contemporary antisemitism and houses a research lab on social media and hate. The program is also advancing several strategic initiatives. These include a major project funded by the National Endowment for the Humanities and led by ISCA and the Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars, Addressing Antisemitism as a Global Challenge: Political, Cultural, and Intellectual Sources and Manifestations , and a new Israel Studies and Business program that connects students with the economic, entrepreneurial, and technological dimensions of contemporary Israel. The program also features an outstanding Hebrew curriculum supported by new technologies, and a strong Yiddish program, along with broad offerings in Jewish history, culture and society. The successful candidate will be a scholar or artist of exceptional distinction, with a PhD or equivalent terminal degree (field-dependent), and a record of original research or creative achievement. Indiana University offers strong support for research and teaching in Jewish Studies. Candidates may come from a wide range of disciplines in the humanities, arts, history and social sciences. The Lou and Sybil Mervis Chair in Jewish Cultural Studies was established to enhance understanding of modern Jewish culture and art, particularly in America, with special attention to Jewish contributions to theater and drama, humor, music, film, literature, radio and television, the visual arts, or related fields. The Mervis Chair provides resources to advance scholarship and creative work, including support for research and related needs. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their application electronically at http://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/31666 . Application materials include a cover letter, curriculum vita, and the names of three references. Applications received before Monday, November 9, 2026 will be assured of consideration, but the search will continue until a suitable candidate is identified. For additional information on the Borns Jewish Studies Program, please see: https://jewishstudies.indiana.edu/index.html . For any questions about the position, please feel free to contact Interim Provost John Ciorciari, the search committee chair, at provost@iu.edu . Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and a provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications The successful candidate will be a scholar or artist of exceptional distinction, with a PhD or equivalent terminal degree (field-dependent), and a record of original research or creative achievement. Indiana University offers strong support for research and teaching in Jewish Studies. Candidates may come from a wide range of disciplines in the humanities, arts, history and social sciences. Department Contact for Questions John Ciorciari, Interim Provost: provost@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. For Best Consideration Date 11/09/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101440-2025 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34263": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology Moyle Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology Moyle Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology Moyle Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology Moyle Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The Moyle Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University, Bloomington is seeking a Research Associate/Lab manager . Our department is a large, unified department with world-class research spanning all levels of biological organization and diverse experimental systems (www.biology.indiana.edu). Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Our lab in Evolution, Ecology, and Behavior investigates the genetic and evolutionary processes that shape speciation and adaptation. We use experiments, observations, and genomic analyses alongside other tools, focusing especially on plant species in the hyperdiverse Solanaceae. The main goal for this position is to conduct research investigating ecological, functional, and genetic variation associated with fruit color transitions, to understand mechanisms of repeated evolution of this important and diverse trait. Basic Qualifications Core job duties include: (1) plant cultivation, husbandry, and measurement and/or preparation for genomic analysis. (2) Testing and troubleshooting new phenotyping techniques. (3) Supervising and mentoring undergraduate and other contributors to the project. (4) Supporting our research compliance associated documentation. (5) Management and analysis of large datasets. (6) Learning, conducting and teaching other lab members our key research protocols. Other general responsibilities include: coordinating shared resources in the lab, managing general lab operations, troubleshooting problems, and participating in lab meetings. The position may also involve travel to research conferences or collaborator labs. Education and Experience Required: Bachelor’s degree in a science-related field. The ideal candidate would have 2 or more years of experience in a research setting, or a Master’s degree that includes research experience. Required skills: Strong organizational, coordination, and time management skills High degree of professionalism Ability to work effectively in a team and independently Proficient communication skills, written and verbal Experience with anticipating and solving technical problems Dependability Preferred skills Hands-on research experience with live organisms (especially plant species) Substantial knowledge of experimental design and management Proficiency in working with large datasets Department Contact for Questions To apply, please submit (1) a cover letter (up to 2 pages) describing your research and educational background, your career goals, why you are interested in this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV or resume, and (3) the names and contact info of two references. This can be submitted at the following link. https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33973 . For questions about the position, contact Leonie Moyle ( lmoyle@iu.edu ). Timing and Compensation: Review of applicants will begin as applications are received, and continue until the position is filled. Ideal start date is October 2026, but there is flexibility on this point. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment for up to 2 years is contingent upon satisfactory performance. Salary is commensurate with experience and starting at $44,950. Full benefits are included. Best consideration date is 8/24/26. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 08/24/2026 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101623-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34255": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Primary Care, Pediatrics and Primary Care )",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Primary Care, Pediatrics and Primary Care ) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Primary Care, Pediatrics and Primary Care ) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Primary Care, Pediatrics and Primary Care ) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Optometry Location Bloomington Position Summary Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Primary Care, Pediatrics and Primary Care ) Indiana University School of Optometry, a leader in clinical education, patient care, and research, is seeking skilled clinicians to join our clinical faculty in Bloomington, Indiana. These are clinical-rank, non-tenure-track faculty positions with a preferred start date of May 1, 2027. Applicants must hold an O.D. or equivalent degree, be eligible for optometric licensure in Indiana, and have completed an accredited residency program or possess equivalent relevant clinical experience. Clinical Faculty Positions — Two Openings The School is seeking candidates for two clinical faculty positions: Primary Care: Provide comprehensive primary eye care in our Primary Care Clinic, practicing to the full scope of Indiana optometric licensure and serving as an initial point of contact within our patient care system. Pediatrics and Primary Care: Provide care in both our Binocular Vision/Pediatrics and Primary Care clinics. Candidates may have particular expertise in pediatrics or binocular vision or may have broader family primary care experience and be comfortable caring for both adult and pediatric patients. Candidates for these positions must demonstrate a commitment to patient-centered care and clinical education and be prepared to participate in the clinical supervision and instruction of optometry students. Applicants may request consideration for either or both positions and should identify their position preference or preferences in their application materials. Clinical and Educational Responsibilities The ideal candidates will be enthusiastic members of our faculty who are committed to patient-centered care, clinical education, and the advancement of the profession. Clinical faculty members actively engage in clinical and didactic teaching and help prepare our students to provide ethical, evidence-based, patient-focused care. Faculty may also have opportunities to participate in collaborative clinical research, community health initiatives, and professional activities at the state and national levels. Primary care clinicians at Indiana University School of Optometry diagnose and manage a broad range of refractive and ocular conditions, including ocular disease, dry eye, and binocular vision disorders. Our Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic provides comprehensive care for pediatric patients and adults with binocular vision disorders. Clinical services include the evaluation and management of myopia, amblyopia, strabismic and non-strabismic binocular vision disorders, pediatric eye disease, post- TBI visual conditions, visual information-processing disorders, and vision therapy. Our specialty clinics provide opportunities for faculty to deliver advanced care while teaching students through direct clinical supervision and instruction. Candidates are expected to contribute to the School’s comprehensive clinical education mission, with specific clinical responsibilities determined by their areas of expertise and the needs of the School. Indiana University and Bloomington The School of Optometry’s didactic program and primary clinical education mission are based in Bloomington. Indiana University School of Optometry maintains a variety of externship and residency affiliations and has an active commitment to community health and education. Indiana University is a major research university founded in 1820, with a broad range of academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Bloomington is located in a beautifully wooded and hilly area of southern Indiana, where cultural and recreational opportunities abound. The community offers short commuting times and extensive opportunities for outdoor recreation, arts and culture, dining, and entertainment. Additional information about Bloomington can be found at www.visitbloomington.com . Questions regarding the positions or application process may be directed to Dr. Todd Peabody at tpeabody@iu.edu . Applications received by October 31, 2026 , will receive full consideration. The searches will remain open until the positions are filled. Salary and rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Basic Qualifications Applicants must hold an O.D. or equivalent degree, be eligible for optometric licensure in Indiana, and have completed an accredited residency program or possess equivalent relevant clinical experience. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process may be directed to Dr. Todd Peabody at tpeabody@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/31/2026 Expected Start Date 05/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101656-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Statement of Research and Teaching Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34254": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Optometry Location Bloomington Position Summary Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Optometry (Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic) Indiana University School of Optometry, a leader in clinical education, patient care, and research, is seeking skilled clinicians to join our clinical faculty in Bloomington, Indiana. These are clinical-rank, non-tenure-track faculty positions with a preferred start date of May 1, 2027. Applicants must hold an O.D. or equivalent degree, be eligible for optometric licensure in Indiana, and have completed an accredited residency program or possess equivalent relevant clinical experience. Candidates for the Chief position must also meet the additional specialty, teaching, and leadership qualifications described below. Chief, Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic — One Opening The School is seeking a faculty member to serve as Chief of the Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic . The Chief will provide clinical and administrative leadership for the clinic while delivering patient-centered care and actively engaging in clinical and didactic education. The Chief will help guide the clinical, educational, and strategic direction of the clinic; coordinate clinical and educational activities; support faculty, staff, and students; promote excellence and consistency in patient care; and contribute to the clinic’s continued development. In addition to the qualifications required of all applicants, candidates for the Chief position must have: Demonstrated advanced clinical expertise in pediatric optometry, binocular vision, or both, with sufficient breadth of experience to provide leadership across the scope of the clinic; Experience teaching, supervising, or mentoring students, residents, or other health professionals in a clinical setting; Demonstrated leadership experience involving a clinic, clinical service, academic program, residency program, health-care team, professional organization, or comparable initiative; and Demonstrated ability to lead collaboratively, coordinate clinical and educational activities, and contribute to clinical program development. Preferred qualifications include prior experience directing or coordinating a clinic, clinical service, residency program, academic program, or interdisciplinary clinical team, as well as a record of professional service or scholarship related to pediatric optometry, binocular vision, or optometric education. Clinical and Educational Responsibilities The ideal candidates will be enthusiastic members of our faculty who are committed to patient-centered care, clinical education, and the advancement of the profession. Clinical faculty members actively engage in clinical and didactic teaching and help prepare our students to provide ethical, evidence-based, patient-focused care. Faculty may also have opportunities to participate in collaborative clinical research, community health initiatives, and professional activities at the state and national levels. Primary care clinicians at Indiana University School of Optometry diagnose and manage a broad range of refractive and ocular conditions, including ocular disease, dry eye, and binocular vision disorders. Our Binocular Vision/Pediatrics Clinic provides comprehensive care for pediatric patients and adults with binocular vision disorders. Clinical services include the evaluation and management of myopia, amblyopia, strabismic and non-strabismic binocular vision disorders, pediatric eye disease, post- TBI visual conditions, visual information-processing disorders, and vision therapy. Our specialty clinics provide opportunities for faculty to deliver advanced care while teaching students through direct clinical supervision and instruction. Candidates are expected to contribute to the School’s comprehensive clinical education mission, with specific clinical responsibilities determined by their areas of expertise and the needs of the School. Indiana University and Bloomington The School of Optometry’s didactic program and primary clinical education mission are based in Bloomington. Indiana University School of Optometry maintains a variety of externship and residency affiliations and has an active commitment to community health and education. Indiana University is a major research university founded in 1820, with a broad range of academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Bloomington is located in a beautifully wooded and hilly area of southern Indiana, where cultural and recreational opportunities abound. The community offers short commuting times and extensive opportunities for outdoor recreation, arts and culture, dining, and entertainment. Additional information about Bloomington can be found at www.visitbloomington.com . Questions regarding the positions or application process may be directed to Dr. Todd Peabody at tpeabody@iu.edu . Applications received by October 31, 2026 , will receive full consideration. The searches will remain open until the positions are filled. Salary and rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Basic Qualifications Applicants must hold an O.D. or equivalent degree, be eligible for optometric licensure in Indiana, and have completed an accredited residency program or possess equivalent relevant clinical experience. Demonstrated advanced clinical expertise in pediatric optometry, binocular vision, or both, with sufficient breadth of experience to provide leadership across the scope of the clinic; Experience teaching, supervising, or mentoring students, residents, or other health professionals in a clinical setting; Demonstrated leadership experience involving a clinic, clinical service, academic program, residency program, health-care team, professional organization, or comparable initiative; and Demonstrated ability to lead collaboratively, coordinate clinical and educational activities, and contribute to clinical program development. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process may be directed to Dr. Todd Peabody at tpeabody@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Preferred qualifications include prior experience directing or coordinating a clinic, clinical service, residency program, academic program, or interdisciplinary clinical team, as well as a record of professional service or scholarship related to pediatric optometry, binocular vision, or optometric education. Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/31/2026 Expected Start Date 05/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101658-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Statement of Research and Teaching Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34248": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Scientist Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Scientist Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications received by September 30th, 2026 will receive full consideration. The anticipated start date for this position is November 1st, 2026 but is negotiable. Applicants should include a CV and cover letter. Posting Details Position Details Title Research Scientist Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Indiana Institute on Disability and Community Location Bloomington Position Summary The Center on Education and Lifelong Learning ( CELL ), within the Indiana Institute on Disability and Community at Indiana University Bloomington, seeks a Research Scientist to support a growing portfolio of projects focused on educational leadership, inclusive practices, implementation support, systems improvement, and positive outcomes for children and youth across Indiana. This position will play a key role in supporting Indiana’s State Personnel Development Grant ( SPDG ), including the CELL School Leadership Initiative ( CSLI ), while also contributing to a broader portfolio of grants, master service agreements, partnerships, and emerging initiatives. The Research Scientist will work collaboratively with CELL staff, educators, school and district leaders, state agencies, families, and community partners to design, implement, and disseminate research and evaluation activities; contribute to grant development and management; and support the translation of research into practice. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (Ph.D., Ed.D., or equivalent terminal degree) in education, educational leadership, special education, public administration, organizational leadership, social sciences, implementation science, or a closely related field. Minimum of five (5) years of professional experience in education, program coordination, leadership development, implementation support, systems improvement, or a related field. Demonstrated experience designing, conducting, implementing, or supporting research, evaluation, or continuous improvement initiatives. Evidence of scholarly writing, technical reports, presentations, publications, or other professional dissemination activities. Demonstrated ability to collaborate on externally funded projects, grants, contracts, or research initiatives. Department Contact for Questions Questions about the position should be addressed to Director, Pennie Gregory ( pmgregor@iu.edu ). Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications: Experience publishing research, evaluation findings, technical assistance products, or practitioner-focused resources. Record of presenting at state, national, or professional conferences. Experience serving as principal investigator, co-principal investigator, project director, or key personnel on grant-funded initiatives. Experience developing grant proposals, securing external funding, or contributing to funded projects. Demonstrated leadership within professional organizations, state initiatives, research centers, or educational improvement efforts. Experience in K–12 education, educational leadership, special education, school improvement, instructional coaching, or related educational settings. Salary and Rank Special Instructions Applications received by September 30th, 2026 will receive full consideration. The anticipated start date for this position is November 1st, 2026 but is negotiable. Applicants should include a CV and cover letter. For Best Consideration Date 09/30/2026 Expected Start Date 11/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101660-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34231": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Biology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Associate, Biology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Associate, Biology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Associate, Biology Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary Research Associate (Laboratory Operations and Coordination) Department: Biology Campus: Bloomington Job Category: Research Job Type: Full-Time Department Information We are seeking an exceptional Laboratory Manager to support a collaborative research environment spanning three active academic laboratories: the Bisson, Limoli, and van Kessel labs . This joint position offers a unique opportunity to work at the center of multiple research programs, helping coordinate operations, people, and resources across a diverse and high-performing scientific team. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Job Summary The Research Associate will support the operational, administrative, and organizational needs of three jointly managed research laboratories. This role is centered on coordination—ensuring that scheduling, communication, logistics, and resource management are aligned across labs. The position is ideal for a candidate who excels in managing complex systems, tracking multiple workflows, and supporting a dynamic research environment through strong organizational practices. Key Responsibilities: Operational Organization Develop and maintain organized systems for lab operations, including shared documents, calendars, and communication platforms Ensure continuity and consistency in workflows across labs Track tasks, deadlines, and responsibilities to support efficient lab functioning Serve as the central liaison between labs and university administrative units (e.g., procurement, facilities, and safety offices) Purchasing and Resource Tracking Coordinate purchasing processes, including non-standard orders and vendor interactions Track inventory levels and ensure availability of lab supplies Monitor standing orders, service agreements, and software licenses Maintain organized records of purchases, shipments, and equipment servicing Coordination and Scheduling Coordinate lab meetings and events Maintain and manage calendars, room reservations, and scheduling systems Organize retreats, seminars, and lab-wide events, including logistics and communications Coordinate travel arrangements, reimbursements, and financial tracking for lab-related activities Personnel Coordination Manage onboarding and offboarding processes, ensuring completion of required steps and access to resources Coordinate schedules and logistics for visiting scholars and job candidates Provide organizational support to lab members and facilitate clear communication across groups Assist in resolving logistical challenges and maintaining a collaborative environment Compliance and Lab Infrastructure Coordinate safety protocols, inspections, and required documentation Organize equipment maintenance, service scheduling, and lab upkeep activities Maintain records related to lab operations, safety compliance, and shared resources Basic Qualifications Qualifications Required: Bachelor’s degree in a scientific, administrative, or related field Experience in a research, laboratory, or academic support environment Strong organizational, coordination, and time management skills Excellent written and verbal communication skills Preferred: Experience supporting multiple teams, projects, or shared research environments Familiarity with university administrative systems and processes Ability to manage competing priorities and work independently Working Conditions / Demands This position operates in a combined laboratory and office environment. Part-time remote options could be possible with certain hiring restrictions. The role requires coordination across multiple lab spaces and interaction with faculty, staff, and students. Additional Information This position is focused on operational coordination rather than independent research. The Research Associate will play a central role in maintaining organization, communication, and efficiency across three collaborating laboratories. Department Contact for Questions Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their qualifications and interest in the position. To apply, please submit (1) a cover letter describing your research and educational background, your career goals, why you are interested in this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV or resume, and (3) the names and contact info of two references. This can be submitted at the following link: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33409 . For questions about the position, contact saltylab@iu.edu Timing and Compensation: Review of applicants will begin soon and continue until the position is filled. Ideal start date is July 1, 2026, but there is flexibility on this point. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment for up to 2 years is contingent upon satisfactory performance. Salary is commensurate with experience and starting at $50,000. Full benefits are included. The best consideration date is 06/16/26. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 06/16/2026 Expected Start Date 07/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101581-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34228": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Danthi Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Danthi Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Danthi Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To apply: You may go to this link; https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34220 . Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your current research and career goals. Please make sure to also include why you think you are suitable for this position (2) a CV or resume and (3) the name and contact information for two references. Timing and compensation: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and continue until the position is filled. Though the ideal start date for the position is January 4, 2027, a different start date is negotiable. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment is contingent upon satisfactory performance. The best consideration date is 11/1/26. Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Danthi Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The department of Biology is a large, unified department with strong undergraduate degrees, nationally-ranked graduate programs, and world-class research spanning the breadth of biological questions and experimental systems – from ecosystems to microbiology and developmental biology, from evolution to cell biology, from molecular biology to systems biology, bioinformatics, and genomics. It is always an exciting time for Biology – enormous advances in global genome analysis coupled with unprecedented developments in interdisciplinary research have made the 21st century the Century of Biology. For more information about the department, you can find it here: About: Department of Biology: Indiana University Bloomington . The Danthi laboratory is seeking a Postdoctoral research fellow for our NIH supported project on the detection of reovirus infection. We investigate how viral and host factor affect the exposure and accumulation of viral RNA in the cell and investigate the consequences of aberrant exposure of viral RNA . The individual hired into this position will continue ongoing research into this area and initiate new projects related to the reovirus-host interactions. The ideal candidate will should have knowledge, experience and confidence to work in independently and help other laboratory members. Basic Qualifications Qualifications and skills required: · PhD in Virology or related discipline · Mastery of standard molecular cloning methods · Mammalian Cell culture · Genome editing · Fluorescence Microscopy · Teamwork skill · Proficiency in written and oral communication Department Contact for Questions For questions about the position, contact Pranav Danthi (pdanthi@iu.edu) Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary will be commensurate with experience, and full benefits are included. Special Instructions To apply: You may go to this link; https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34220 . Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your current research and career goals. Please make sure to also include why you think you are suitable for this position (2) a CV or resume and (3) the name and contact information for two references. Timing and compensation: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and continue until the position is filled. Though the ideal start date for the position is January 4, 2027, a different start date is negotiable. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment is contingent upon satisfactory performance. The best consideration date is 11/1/26. For Best Consideration Date 11/01/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101657-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34227": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Biology Dharan Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Associate, Biology Dharan Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Associate, Biology Dharan Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Associate, Biology Dharan Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The Dharan Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University Bloomington is seeking a highly motivated research associate to support ongoing research projects focused on HIV -1 biology and to assist with general laboratory operations. The research associate will work closely with the Principal Investigator and other lab members on active research projects while also helping with day-to-day laboratory activities. Responsibilities will include cell culture, preparation of laboratory reagents, maintaining laboratory stocks, ordering supplies, organizing laboratory materials, and assisting with routine experiments. The successful candidate will work in a collaborative research environment and may also assist with the training and supervision of undergraduate researchers. Basic Qualifications A bachelor’s degree in biology, microbiology, molecular biology, biotechnology, or a related field is required. Previous laboratory research experience is preferred but not required. Preference will be given to candidates who are interested in making a longer-term commitment to the lab and anticipate staying in the position for more than one year. Responsibilities Assist the PI and lab members with ongoing HIV -1 research projects. Perform routine mammalian cell culture and laboratory experiments. Prepare buffers, media, reagents, and other laboratory solutions. Maintain laboratory supplies, inventories, and place orders as needed. Maintain organized laboratory records and follow established protocols. Assist with general laboratory organization and equipment maintenance. Help train and supervise undergraduate researchers when appropriate. Work collaboratively with graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and other laboratory members. Learn and troubleshoot new experimental techniques as needed. Required Skills Strong organizational and time-management skills. Ability to work independently after initial training. Good written and verbal communication skills. Attention to detail and ability to maintain accurate laboratory records. Ability to anticipate and troubleshoot routine laboratory problems. High degree of initiative, reliability, and professionalism. Willingness to learn new laboratory techniques. Department Contact for Questions To apply , please submit (1) a cover letter describing your educational background, your career goals, why you think you are suitable for this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV or resume and (3) the name and contact info of two references. Applications and documents can be submitted at this link: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34054 . For questions about the position, contact Adarsh Dharan ( adharan@iu.edu ). Timing and compensation: Review of applicants will begin soon and continue until this position is filled. The ideal start date is October 1st, 2026. The initial appointment is for one year, with an annual salary of $43,888 and full benefits. Reappointment beyond the initial one-year period is possible based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding. Future salary increases will be dependent on performance and available funding. Best consideration date is 9/1/26. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/01/2026 Expected Start Date 10/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101634-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34226": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Ye Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow - Ye Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow - Ye Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The initial appointment for this position will be for 12 months. Additional 12-month extension will be available based on performance. A competitive salary along with a benefits package will be offered. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to http://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/32977 . Applications will be reviewed upon receipt. Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Ye Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Chemistry Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Chemistry in the College of Arts + Sciences at Indiana University, consists of a well-rounded research community with cutting-edge expertise in a wide variety of modern disciplines including analytical chemistry, organic chemistry, inorganic chemistry, physical chemistry, materials chemistry, and biochemistry. A postdoc position is available for the design, synthesis, and electron microscopy characterization of nanomaterials for applications in heterogenous catalysis and plasmonics. In this federally funded project, the postdoc will collaborate with scientists at other US institutions and National Laboratories to pursue cutting-edge scientific research. The incumbent will have the opportunity to participate in professional development activities according to their professional goals. These include grant writing opportunities, seminars and conference attendance. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in chemistry, materials science, chemical engineering or related field by the time of starting this position. Preferred Qualifications: The ideal candidate will be an expert in one of the following areas including but not limited to: (1) nanocrystal synthesis and characterization, (2) electrochemistry, (3) catalysis, (4) analytical or in-situ electron microscopy. The candidate will possess a creative, analytical approach to problem solving, the ability to work independently and the willingness to engage in interdisciplinary collaborations. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to: Prof. Xingchen Ye, Department of Chemistry, Indiana University, 800 E. Kirkwood Avenue, Bloomington, IN 47405, xingye@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank $48,000.00/Postdoctoral Fellow Special Instructions The initial appointment for this position will be for 12 months. Additional 12-month extension will be available based on performance. A competitive salary along with a benefits package will be offered. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to http://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/32977 . Applications will be reviewed upon receipt. For Best Consideration Date 05/04/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101553-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34203": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Chemistry Lewis Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow, Chemistry Lewis Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow, Chemistry Lewis Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Chemistry Lewis Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Chemistry Location Bloomington Position Summary Department Information: The Department of Chemistry in the College of Arts + Sciences at Indiana University, consists of a well-rounded research community with cutting-edge expertise in a wide variety of modern disciplines including analytical chemistry, organic chemistry, inorganic chemistry, physical chemistry, materials chemistry, and biochemistry. Indiana University is an equal employment and affirmative action employer and a provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Job Summary: The Lewis Lab in the Department of Chemistry at Indiana University is accepting applications for a Postdoctoral Fellow position. The position offers an opportunity to develop and apply cutting- edge protein engineering techniques to address fundamental challenges in synthetic chemistry and biocatalysis. The successful applicant will use a deep understanding of organic and organometallic chemistry, combined with state-of-the-art methods generative protein design, directed evolution, next gen sequencing, and laboratory automation to develop B12-dependent enzymes or artificial metalloenzymes containing different organometallic cofactors for new chemical transformations. Motivated applicants with PhD training in any area of chemistry or biochemistry will be considered. Experience with either molecular biology or synthetic chemistry is strongly preferred. The Lewis Lab is a dynamic multidisciplinary laboratory environment. The successful applicant will play an integral role within our growing team of chemists, biochemists, and protein engineers and will emerge with a skill set that is equally relevant to academia and industry. Trainees receive regular guidance and feedback to ensure progress is made toward their career goals, whatever those may be. Trainees are also afforded the scheduling flexibility needed to maintain an appropriate work life balance. Responsibilities: Primary research responsibilities will include cloning and other molecular biology required for enzyme production and engineering, synthesis of substrates and product standards, high throughput screening of enzyme libraries, and application of computational protein design tools. Careful documentation of all findings, formal research reports and presentations, and participation in group equipment/facility maintenance is also expected. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications: Candidates must have extensive experience with either molecular biology or synthetic chemistry. PhD in any area of chemistry or biochemistry. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with both molecular biology and synthetic chemistry is preferred but not required. Familiarity with computational design tools, organometallic catalysis/methodology development, enzyme library generation and screening are highly desirable. Department Contact for Questions The initial appointment for this position will be for 12 months. Additional 12-month extension will be available based on performance. A competitive salary along with a benefits package will be offered. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33690 . Applications will be reviewed upon receipt. Best consideration date: 7/21/2026. Expected start date is 10/1/26. Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to: Prof. Lewis, Department of Chemistry, Indiana University, 800 E. Kirkwood Avenue, Bloomington, IN 47405, jcl3@iu.edu. For additional information visit: https://lewis.lab.indiana.edu/ Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 07/21/2026 Expected Start Date 10/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101599-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34202": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Biology Fuqua Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Associate, Biology Fuqua Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Associate, Biology Fuqua Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Associate, Biology Fuqua Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Biology is a large, unified department with strong undergraduate degrees, nationally-ranked graduate programs, and world-class research spanning the breadth of biological questions and experimental systems – from ecosystems to microbiology and developmental biology, from evolution to cell biology, from molecular biology to systems biology, bioinformatics, and genomics. It is always an exciting time for Biology – enormous advances in global genome analysis coupled with unprecedented developments in interdisciplinary research have made the 21st century the Century of Biology. For more information about the department, you can find it here: About: Department of Biology: Indiana University Bloomington . The Fuqua Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University, Bloomington is seeking a Research Associate. Our lab in the Microbiology section is focused on complex bacterial behaviors, including biofilm formation and host interactions, using the plant pathogen Agrobacterium tumefaciens as a model. The overarching goal for this position is to make timely progress on our NIH grant entitled “Mechanisms of pterin-dependent regulation in proteobacterial systems” (Grant number R01 GM160733) which explores a new regulatory mechanism that controls biofilm formation we have discovered based on excreted metabolites called pterins (for details see Greenwich et al. 2024; PNAS , doi:10.1073/pnas.2319903121). The Fuqua lab research team is currently comprised of graduate students, a senior research scientist, and undergraduates. All members of our team contribute to the projects of other team members -this may include assistance with a research protocol, feedback on presentations, edits on manuscripts, and so on. The lab philosophy is that a collaborative and supportive research environment benefits everyone involved. We are seeking an individual to join this team, contribute to the lab in general, and further the goals of the NIH grant. Basic Qualifications Core job duties include: (1) Learning, conducting and teaching other lab members our key research protocols. In the lab, this will include: DNA purification, Molecular Cloning, Gel Electrophoresis, DNA Sequencing, Polymerase Chain Reaction, Bacterial Transformation, Microbiological Cultivation, SDS - PAGE , Western Blotting. These and other experimental approaches will be applied as needed to the experimental plan for the pterin project. (2) Management of pterin grant meetings and material exchanges between collaborating laboratories. (3) Laboratory management and ordering. (4) Participation in general laboratory task rotations (5) Written summaries of research (for annual grant reporting and for manuscript preparation), and research presentations (lab meetings, grant meetings, and conferences). Required skills · Effective time management · Accurate and organized record keeping · Ability to work in a team · Receptivity to training and new techniques · Proficiency in written and oral communication · Problem solving and pro-active approach to preventing problems · Dependability and punctuality · Flexibility for research tasks and prioritization of experiments. Preferred skills · Molecular biology experience · DNA sequence analysis · Aseptic technique and microbiology · Familiarity with data analysis and statistics Working conditions: Laboratory research with periods of intense focus and technical clarity. Organization, planning and record keeping for experiments are crucial and this work requires careful and deliberate effort. Effective communication between lab members is critical. Department Contact for Questions To apply: Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your interests and background, why you are interested in the position, and the date you are able to start, (2) a CV or resume, (3) names and contact information for two references. This can be submitted to the following link: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33480 . For questions about the position, contact Clay Fuqua ( cfuqua@iu.edu ). Timing and compensation: Applicant review will begin on June 1, 2026. The initial appointment is for one year, with reappointment contingent on performance and continued funding. Salary will be commensurate with experience and full benefits are included. Best consideration date is 6/16/26 and expected start date is 7/1/26. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 06/16/2026 Expected Start Date 07/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101580-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34147": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Bassoon); Tenure or Tenure-track position (faculty)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Bassoon); Tenure or Tenure-track position (faculty) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Bassoon); Tenure or Tenure-track position (faculty) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. A complete application consists of a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and information about three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For candidates who advance in this search, an email message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your provided references. Candidates who advance in this search may also be asked to provide audio/video recordings. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration, however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Bassoon); Tenure or Tenure-track position (faculty) Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Woodwinds Location Bloomington Position Summary The Jacobs School of Music seeks candidates for a position in the Woodwind Department in Bassoon. The ideal candidate will have recognition both nationally and internationally as a performer and as a pedagogue in a wide variety of settings. Primary responsibilities include building and maintaining a studio of undergraduate- and graduate-level bassoon students, teaching studio classes and reed making classes, and coaching chamber ensembles. Additional duties could include teaching orchestra literature classes, pedagogy classes, and other related courses. The successful candidate is expected to participate in departmental meetings, recruitment activities and auditions, doctoral committees, and recital hearing committees, along with other department activities, such as faculty recitals and outreach activities, master classes, service on Jacobs School of Music committees, and other similar activities. Basic Qualifications The ideal candidate must have an established or growing reputation with broad professional experience and have the ability to work successfully with gifted and advanced university-level students. Master’s degree in music is required, or an equivalent of outstanding professional experience will also be considered. Department Contact for Questions Rachel Patrick, Office of the Executive Associate Dean: (812) 855-5541; rnpatric@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary and rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Special Instructions A complete application consists of a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and information about three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For candidates who advance in this search, an email message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your provided references. Candidates who advance in this search may also be asked to provide audio/video recordings. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration, however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. For Best Consideration Date 09/26/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101645-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
