    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34823": {
      "title": "Robert E. and Avis Tarrant Burke Postdoctoral Fellow in Ancient and Indigenous Art of the Americas",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Robert E. and Avis Tarrant Burke Postdoctoral Fellow in Ancient and Indigenous Art of the Americas Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Robert E. and Avis Tarrant Burke Postdoctoral Fellow in Ancient and Indigenous Art of the Americas Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Robert E. and Avis Tarrant Burke Postdoctoral Fellow in Ancient and Indigenous Art of the Americas Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Fine Arts - History Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Art History at Indiana University, Bloomington ( https://arthistory.indiana.edu/ ) invites applications for the Robert E. and Avis Tarrant Burke Postdoctoral Fellowship in Ancient and/or Indigenous Art of the Americas t o begin in August 2027. The duration of the fellowship is two (2) years. We welcome applications from early career scholars specializing in the art, architecture, archaeology and/or material culture of the ancient and indigenous peoples of North, Central, and/or South America, including the Caribbean. Eligible candidates will have received their Ph.D. degree within 7 years of the position start date. H1-B sponsorship is not available for this position. We seek a dynamic and original thinker who is committed to excellence in scholarship and is well-suited to teaching an introductory survey and an upper-level course for undergraduate and graduate students. The teaching load is one (1) course per semester. The salary is $58,000 plus benefits. Applicants should submit a cover letter that includes a detailed description of their research program and teaching philosophy; a CV; a writing sample (between 20-30 pages), and the names and contact information for three (3) referees. Applications should be submitted online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34561 . Review of applications will begin on 15 January 2027. Queries about the position can be sent to the search committee chair, Cordula Grewe ( cgrewe@iu.edu ). The Burke Postdoctoral Fellow will join a vibrant community of scholars and students working across a wide range of topics in the history of art and visual and material culture. With our strong commitment to interdisciplinarity, we offer not only a supportive environment in which to build the foundation of a career. We also have strong connections with allied institutes, departments, and programs across the Bloomington campus—such as African American and African Diaspora Studies; American Studies; Anthropology; Folklore and Ethnomusicology; History; Latin American and Caribbean Studies; Latino Studies; or Native American and Indigenous Studies. The Burke Postdoctoral Fellow will also be able to forge close working relationships with the Sidney and Lois Eskenazi Museum of Art; the Lilly Library; the IU Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology; and the Kinsey Institute. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications Doctorate degree in the field of art history with a specialty in the area of Ancient and Indigenous Art of the Americas conferred within 7 years of the position’s start date. Department Contact for Questions Professor Cordula Grewe cgrewe@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank $58,000 plus $1,000 moving supplement and $2,000 in research funding Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/15/2027 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101719-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Writing Sample Other 4 Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34822": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Clarinet); Tenured or Tenure-track position (Faculty)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Clarinet); Tenured or Tenure-track position (Faculty) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Clarinet); Tenured or Tenure-track position (Faculty) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. A complete application consists of a letter of application and curriculum vitae, along with contact information for three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For candidates who advance in this search, an email message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your provided references. Candidates who advance in this search will also be asked to provide audio/video recordings. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration; however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Music (Clarinet); Tenured or Tenure-track position (Faculty) Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Woodwinds Location Bloomington Position Summary The Jacobs School of Music seeks candidates for a position in the Woodwind Department in Clarinet. The ideal candidate will have recognition both nationally and internationally as a performer and as a pedagogue in a wide variety of settings. Primary responsibilities include building and maintaining a studio of undergraduate- and graduate-level clarinet students, teaching studio classes, and coaching chamber ensembles. Additional duties could include teaching orchestra literature classes, pedagogy classes, and other related courses. The successful candidate is expected to participate in departmental meetings, recruitment activities and auditions, doctoral committees, and recital hearing committees, along with other department activities, such as faculty recitals, outreach activities, master classes, service on Jacobs School of Music committees, and other similar activities. Basic Qualifications The ideal candidate must have an established or growing reputation with broad professional experience and have the ability to work successfully with gifted and advanced university-level students. Master’s degree in music or related field is required and a doctorate is preferred. Commensurate professional experience will also be considered. Department Contact for Questions Rachel Patrick, Office of the Executive Associate Dean: (812) 855-5541 rnpatric@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Salary and rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Special Instructions A complete application consists of a letter of application and curriculum vitae, along with contact information for three professional references. You will be asked to provide the name, title, e-mail address, and other information for each of your reference providers. For candidates who advance in this search, an email message with instructions about uploading letters of reference will be sent automatically to each of your provided references. Candidates who advance in this search will also be asked to provide audio/video recordings. Please do not send audio/video files or other materials until requested to do so. If you need help completing your application, contact Rachel Patrick, (812) 855-5541, rnpatric@iu.edu . Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Deadline: Applications received by September 26, 2026 will be assured full consideration; however the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. For Best Consideration Date 09/26/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101643-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34817": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Appointee",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Post-Doctoral Appointee Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Post-Doctoral Appointee Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Post-Doctoral Appointee Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Gill Institute for Neuroscience Location Bloomington Position Summary The Gill Institute for Neuroscience with Indiana University Bloomington is seeking applications for a Post-Doctoral Appointee. The broad duties and responsibilities of this position include designing, planning, executing, and analyzing experiments with mice investigating the consequences of neuronal circuit manipulation, training graduate students, preparing reports and presenting experimental results to the IU Bloomington and scientific communities. Basic Qualifications Position requires a Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Biomedical Science or related field. Experience in either of the following techniques is required: neuroanatomical techniques, in vitro or in vivo electrophysiology, behavioral tests using mice, AAV injection and/or fiber photometry. Department Contact for Questions Questions about the position should be addressed to Dr. Norbert Hajos, nhajos@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Knowledge of using Clampex, Matlab, Origin, DeepLabCut or similar software is an advantage. Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/15/2026 Expected Start Date 11/01/2026 Posting Number IU-101682-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Will you now or in the future require employer sponsorship for employment (e.g. H-1B status)? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 List Of References Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34805": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Han Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Han Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Han Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Biology Han Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The department of Biology is a large, unified department with strong undergraduate degrees, nationally-ranked graduate programs, and world-class research spanning the breadth of biological questions and experimental systems – from ecosystems to microbiology and developmental biology, from evolution to cell biology, from molecular biology to systems biology, bioinformatics, genomics, and biotechnologies. It is always an exciting time for Biology – enormous advances in global genome analysis coupled with unprecedented developments in interdisciplinary research have made the 21st century the Century of Biology. For more information about the department, you can find it here: About: Department of Biology: Indiana University Bloomington . Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications The Han Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University, Bloomington is seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher. Our lab focuses on studying the biology of spatial RNA localization in neurons using cell lines, Drosophila , and mice as biological systems. We develop cutting-edge tools and technologies at the interface of molecular biology, chemical biology, and CRISPR for RNA localization profiling, imaging, as well as manipulation. More about Han Lab Research can be found at our lab website . The overarching goal for this position is to make timely progress on our NIH DP1 project on Interrogating RNA localization and local translation during synaptic plasticity induced by opioid use disorder . We welcome talented, creative, and highly motivated individuals who thrive in an exciting, collaborative research environment. Core job duties include: (1) To set up and maintain the oral oxycodone self-administration mouse model. This is an established mouse model, and we need to maintain 20-30 modeled mice at a time. The candidate must have experience in mouse experiments including brain dissection, immunofluorescence imaging of mouse brain slices, and AAV injection of the mouse brain. (2) To perform synaptosome isolation (protocol has been established) and subsequent RNA /protein analysis. A candidate with experience of RNA experiments (smFISH and RNA -seq) is preferred. (3) To mentor graduate students and teach other lab members on mouse experiments and techniques. The candidate must show strong independent research skills (literature reading, experimental design, data analysis, and troubleshooting) and collaborative working style. (4) To draft manuscripts, animal protocols, and research proposals. The candidate must be honest, responsible, and efficient in research. Education requirement: The candidate must have obtained a Ph.D. in Biology or a related field or be on track to complete the degree within the coming year. Ph.D. must be obtained before position can begin. Required skills: · Mouse breeding, maintenance, and brain dissection · Mouse brain slice staining and imaging · AAV injection of mouse brains Preferred skills: · Synaptosome preparation from mouse brains · RNA purification and library prep · RNA -seq data analysis · Substance-use-disorder-related research experience Department Contact for Questions To apply , please submit (1) a cover letter describing your research and educational background, your career goals, why you are interested in this position, and the date you are available to start, (2) a CV or resume including lists of publications, research experience, and skills, and (3) the names and contact info of three references. Please submit the required documents at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34774 . For questions about the position, please contact Dr. Mengting Han ( hanmt@iu.edu ) or Dr. Rongbing Huang (rohuang@iu.edu). Timing and Compensation: Review of applicants will begin December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Ideal start date is June 1, 2027. The initial appointment is for 1 year, and reappointment for up to 4 years is contingent upon satisfactory performance. Salary and benefits will be provided in accordance with NIH and Indiana University postdoctoral guidelines with a salary of $50,000-$60,000 depending on experience. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 06/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101753-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34801": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor in Audiology",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant Professor in Audiology Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant Professor in Audiology Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant Professor in Audiology Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Speech & Hearing Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences at Indiana University invites applications for a 12-month, full-time (non-tenure track) Clinical Assistant Professor in Audiology. The responsibilities for this position include: (1) clinical supervision of AuD students and provision of audiological services; (2) classroom teaching of clinical courses that may include teaching or team-teaching in an area of expertise; and (3) service to the department, university and professional communities. The ideal candidate will have expertise in pediatric audiology , although candidates with expertise in other areas of audiology will also be considered. Indiana University is a highly regarded public research university. The undergraduate and graduate programs of the Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences are ranked among the top in the nation. The Bloomington campus, home to approximately 42,000 students, is a one-hour drive south of Indianapolis. Our department is housed in a state-of-the art facility, the Regional Academic Health Center, with the Speech-Language and Hearing Clinics, numerous research laboratories, and is connected to the IU Health Bloomington hospital. The IU Hearing Clinic within the department provides a robust variety of clinical services to the Bloomington community, including electrophysiology, diagnostic assessment of pediatric and adult patients, evaluation and programming of implantable devices, vestibular testing, and hearing aid fitting and verification. The department also has a preschool program aimed at supporting language development in preschool-aged children, including those who are typically developing, learning English as a second language, or experiencing communication delays. The proximity to the hospital and collocation with other units in the building (nursing, social work, and medical sciences) fosters collaborations among faculty, provides interprofessional education for students, and facilitates patient-oriented research. The departmental climate is one of collaboration both within and across the SLHS disciplines, as many faculty co-teach courses and collaborate on research and community outreach. Bloomington is a collegial community with a vibrant arts and cultural scene, varied dining options, and scenic outdoor spaces. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications Required: An AuD or PhD in audiology, ASHA certification ( CCC -A), and Indiana state licensure in audiology by the projected date of employment (5/1/2027). Preferred: Prior student supervision experience, experience with classroom teaching. Department Contact for Questions Jennifer Lentz, PhD Email: jjlentz @iu.edu Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Indiana University 2631 East Discovery Parkway Bloomington, IN 47408 Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/11/2027 Expected Start Date 05/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101749-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34800": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant Professor Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant Professor Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant Professor Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Speech & Hearing Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences ( SLHS ) at Indiana University Bloomington ( https://slhs.indiana.edu/ ) invites applications for a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure-track position at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor beginning July 1, 2027 . We seek a highly motivated clinical educator with expertise in preschool aged speech and language delays/disorders . We are particularly interested in candidates who have experience with autism, alternative-augmentative communication ( AAC ) and/or speech sound disorders. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in graduate clinical teaching, student mentoring, and service to the department and profession. Primary responsibilities will include clinical supervision of MA SLP graduate clinicians within the university preschool program and university-based clinic. Additional responsibilities include: Application of accreditation standards across clinical experiences Service to the department, university, and professional communities Potential for classroom teaching/co-teaching Indiana University is a highly regarded public research university. The undergraduate and graduate programs of the Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences are ranked among the top in the nation. The Bloomington campus, home to approximately 42,000 students, is a one-hour drive south of Indianapolis. Our department is housed in a state-of-the art facility, the Regional Academic Health Center, with the Speech-Language and Hearing Clinics, numerous research laboratories, and is connected to the IU Health Bloomington Hospital. The department also offers a preschool program aimed at supporting language development in preschool-aged children, including those who are typically developing, learning English as a second language, or experiencing communication delays. The proximity to the hospital and collocation with other units in the building (nursing, social work, and medical sciences) fosters collaborations among faculty, provides interprofessional education for students, and facilitates patient-oriented research. The department’s faculty are highly collaborative both within and across the SLHS disciplines, and many co-teach courses and collaborate on research and community outreach. Bloomington is a collegial community with a vibrant arts and cultural scene, varied dining options, and scenic outdoor spaces. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications A master’s degree or PhD in Speech-Language Pathology, ASHA certification ( CCC - SLP ), clinical experience in assessment and treatment of preschool aged speech and language disorders and AAC , 2 hours of professional development in supervision-post certification, at least three years of supervisory experience, and an Indiana licensure in speech-language pathology by the start of employment. Candidates should have demonstrated effectiveness as educators, strong communication skills, and a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Department Contact for Questions Erin Peabody, M.A., CCC - SLP Clinical Associate Professor IU Bloomington Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences ecolone@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 01/04/2027 Expected Start Date 07/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101747-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34798": {
      "title": "Lecturer Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Lecturer Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Lecturer Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Lecturer Position in Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Speech & Hearing Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences ( SLHS ) at Indiana University Bloomington (https://sphs.indiana.edu/) invites applications for a full-time, 10-month, non-tenure-track position at the rank of Lecturer beginning August 1, 2027 . We seek an outstanding educator with expertise in speech and language sciences , with a particular interest in candidates who can contribute to instruction in phonological disorders and related areas in speech and language. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, student mentoring, and service to the department and profession. Primary responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. Service activities may include curriculum development and participation in departmental initiatives. Indiana University is a highly regarded public research university. The undergraduate and graduate programs of the Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences are ranked among the top in the nation. The Bloomington campus, home to approximately 42,000 students, is a one-hour drive south of Indianapolis. Our department is housed in a state-of-the art facility, the Regional Academic Health Center, with the Speech-Language and Hearing Clinics, numerous research laboratories, and is connected to the Bloomington hospital. The department also offers a preschool program aimed at supporting language development in preschool-aged children, including those who are typically developing, learning English as a second language, or experiencing communication delays. The proximity to the hospital and collocation with other units in the building (nursing, social work, and medical sciences) fosters collaborations among faculty, provides interprofessional education for students, and facilitates patient-oriented research. The departmental climate is one of collaboration both within and across the SLHS disciplines, as many faculty co-teach courses and collaborate on research and community outreach. Bloomington is a collegial community with a vibrant arts and cultural scene, varied dining options, and scenic outdoor spaces. Salary is competitive and commensurate with level of experience and qualifications. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Basic Qualifications A Ph.D. in Speech-Language Pathology, Communication Sciences and Disorders, Linguistics, or a closely related field is preferred , though applicants with an earned master’s degree and substantial evidence of excellence in teaching will also be considered. Candidates should have demonstrated effectiveness as educators, strong communication skills, and a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Department Contact for Questions Brielle C. Stark, PhD Chair, Lecturer Search Committee Department of Speech, Language and Hearing Sciences Indiana University Bloomington bcstark@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101746-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
