    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34936": {
      "title": "Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University Bloomington - Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University Bloomington - Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University Bloomington - Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University Bloomington - Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Jewish Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary Title: Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies Appointment Status: Non-Tenure Track Department/Program: Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism ( ISCA ) / Borns Jewish Studies Program, Indiana University Bloomington Location: Bloomington, Indiana Term: One-year appointment with the possibility of renewal for a second year Salary & Support: $57,000 plus health benefits Position Summary: The Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism ( ISCA ) in the Borns Jewish Studies Program at Indiana University Bloomington invites applications for a Visiting Fellow in Antisemitism Studies. ISCA is one of the leading research institutes dedicated to the scholarly study of contemporary antisemitism, and this fellowship offers an exceptional opportunity to pursue original research in the field while contributing to the intellectual life of the institute and the broader program. The fellowship is open to scholars working in any discipline and using any methodological approach, including but not limited to historical, sociological, computational, psychological, legal, literary, or educational perspectives on antisemitism. We particularly welcome candidates whose work addresses contemporary manifestations of antisemitism in Europe, North America, the Middle East, or online environments, and whose research complements and enriches ISCA’s existing portfolio of projects. Position is expected to begin January 1, 2027, for a one-year term, with the possibility of renewal for a second year. For more information about ISCA and its current research, please visit: https://isca.indiana.edu Core Responsibilities: Research Pursue an independent, original research agenda in antisemitism studies, resulting in publications, conference presentations, or other scholarly outputs. Participate actively in ISCA seminars, colloquia, research workshops, and collaborative discussions. Contribute to ISCA’s public-facing activities, including events, reports, and media engagement as appropriate. Collaborate with ISCA faculty and research staff on ongoing and emerging projects. Teaching Teach one course per semester on antisemitism or a closely related topic, at the undergraduate or graduate level, in coordination with the Borns Jewish Studies Program. Community and Program Engagement Participate in the intellectual community of the Borns Jewish Studies Program through lectures, workshops, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Contribute to grant writing and funding applications in support of ISCA’s research mission as appropriate. Mentor graduate and undergraduate students engaged in antisemitism-related research where relevant. Qualifications: Required: PhD in a relevant field. The PhD must be completed by the start of the appointment. Demonstrated research focus on antisemitism. Strong record of scholarly publication or a clear publication trajectory based on dissertation or postdoctoral work. Ability to work collegially in an interdisciplinary research environment. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Preferred: Regional or thematic expertise in contemporary antisemitism (e.g., Europe, North America, online/digital environments, the Middle East, campus contexts). Experience with or interest in public-facing scholarship, policy engagement, or applied research. Proficiency in languages relevant to the study of antisemitism Prior teaching experience at the university level. Applications Materials: To apply, please submit the following through Indiana University’s PeopleAdmin system at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34103 A cover letter describing your research agenda, scholarly background, and why you are interested in this fellowship, including your available start date. A current curriculum vitae. A writing sample (article, dissertation chapter, or equivalent, up to 40 pages). A brief teaching statement (1–2 pages), including a proposed course on antisemitism. Contact information for three references. For questions about the position, contact Gunther Jikeli ( gjikeli@iu.edu ). Timing: Review of applications will begin September 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Basic Qualifications Required: PhD in a relevant field. The PhD must be completed by the start of the appointment. Demonstrated research focus on antisemitism. Strong record of scholarly publication or a clear publication trajectory based on dissertation or postdoctoral work. Ability to work collegially in an interdisciplinary research environment. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Department Contact for Questions For questions about the position, contact Gunther Jikeli ( gjikeli@iu.edu ) Additional Qualifications Preferred: Regional or thematic expertise in contemporary antisemitism (e.g., Europe, North America, online/digital environments, the Middle East, campus contexts). Experience with or interest in public-facing scholarship, policy engagement, or applied research. Proficiency in languages relevant to the study of antisemitism Prior teaching experience at the university level. Salary and Rank Salary: $57,000 ; Rank: Visiting Faculty/Scholar (FVX) ; RSP / FV5 Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/15/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101639-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Other Other 2 Other 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34928": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Capone Lab - ENVR",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Fellow - Capone Lab - ENVR Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Fellow - Capone Lab - ENVR Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Provide CV, cover letter summarizing research interests and fit for position, writing sample, and a list of three references. Interested candidates should submit applications at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34329 . Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Capone Lab - ENVR Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Environmental and Occupational Health Location Bloomington Position Summary A postdoctoral fellow position is available in the Capone Lab in the Department of Environmental and Occupational Health at the Indiana University School of Public Health-Bloomington. The fellow will join a multidisciplinary team conducting a five-year CDC -funded study focused on developing and applying integrated surveillance approaches for soil-transmitted helminth ( STH ) infection in the southeastern United States. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis, but applications received by October 5, 2026 will be given full consideration, and the search will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. The earliest anticipated start date is October 19, 2026. Dr. Drew Capone’s research group ( https://caponelab.publichealth.indiana.edu ) has broad interests in environmental health microbiology. Current projects include public health surveillance using fecal waste streams, microbial fate and transport, the role of infrastructure in interrupting enteric pathogen transmission, and predicting public health outcomes using quantitative microbial risk assessment. Lab members conduct field research in Mozambique and Indiana, as well as wet lab work in Bloomington, Indiana. We use tools from engineering, epidemiology, and environmental health microbiology to investigate the problems affecting low-income individuals and aim to improve public health in the communities where these people live. Basic Qualifications The postdoctoral fellow position requires a PhD in environmental health, environmental engineering, environmental science, microbiology, or a related scientific field by the time of appointment. We are looking for candidates with experience in several of the following areas (prior experience with helminths is not required): Experience with project management Experience with molecular microbiology methods (e.g., nucleic acid extraction, qPCR, dPCR) and quantitative data analysis Fieldwork experience, including environmental sampling Strong written and oral communication skills An interest in mentoring undergraduate, master’s, and Ph.D. students Department Contact for Questions Questions pertaining to the PeopleAdmin application procedure may be directed to dwarcher@iu.edu . Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Capone at dscapone@iu.edu Additional Qualifications This position offers a unique opportunity to work at the intersection of environmental microbiology, parasitology, epidemiology, and public health surveillance. The project integrates human and environmental samples (i.e., wastewater, septage, soil, flies, and stool) and uses microscopy and molecular methods, including digital PCR , to investigate STH infection and transmission. The fellow will play a leading scientific role in the project while also coordinating laboratory activities and helping manage this large, collaborative study. The fellow will have opportunities to lead studies and peer-reviewed publications, develop and optimize laboratory methods, analyze complex environmental and epidemiologic data, mentor students, and develop independent research questions and proposals. The position includes collaboration with microbiologists, virologists, environmental engineers, entomologists, biologists, and epidemiologists, as well as travel to study sites in Virginia and Alabama. Opportunities may also be available to participate in other domestic and international projects in the Capone Lab. We are particularly interested in candidates who want to develop an independent research profile in environmental health microbiology and who enjoy working across laboratory, field, and quantitative research. Resources are available for conference travel, and the fellow will have access to formal training in grant writing, research ethics, rigor and reproducibility, and research management. Salary and Rank Special Instructions Provide CV, cover letter summarizing research interests and fit for position, writing sample, and a list of three references. Interested candidates should submit applications at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34329 . For Best Consideration Date 10/05/2026 Expected Start Date 10/19/2026 Posting Number IU-101679-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Other Other 2 Other 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34924": {
      "title": "Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program - Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies (Research Professor)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program - Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies (Research Professor) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program - Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies (Research Professor) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism (ISCA) / Borns Jewish Studies Program - Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies (Research Professor) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Jewish Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary Title: Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies (Research Professor) Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track (Research Professor), Open Rank – Senior Preferred Department Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism ( ISCA ) Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program Indiana University Bloomington Location Bloomington, Indiana Position Summary The Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism ( ISCA ), housed within the Robert A. and Sandra S. Borns Jewish Studies Program at Indiana University Bloomington, invites applications for the Leslie J. Raffel Chair in Antisemitism Studies. The appointment will be at the rank of Research Professor (open rank, with preference for associate or full professor). It is expected to begin January 1, 2027, for a term of up to five years, subject to review, with potential consideration for renewal. The Raffel Chair represents a significant investment in advancing Indiana University’s global leadership in the interdisciplinary study of antisemitism. We seek a widely recognized scholar, in any academic discipline, whose work addresses the most urgent contemporary forms of antisemitism. The occupant of the Chair is expected to be fully conversant with the long history of anti-Jewish hostility while maintaining a primary focus on the most consequential present-day expressions of hostility directed against Jews, Judaism, and the State of Israel. The Chair will provide intellectual leadership at the highest level—strengthening research initiatives, convening scholars across disciplines, expanding strategic partnerships, and pursuing external funding to advance major projects. This is a full-time research appointment. Basic Qualifications • Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field • An established record of significant scholarly achievement focused on antisemitism • Demonstrated expertise in contemporary manifestations of antisemitism • Evidence of sustained research productivity Preferred Qualifications • Senior scholarly standing • Record of successful external grant funding • Demonstrated capacity for intellectual leadership and interdisciplinary collaboration • Experience mentoring graduate students and advanced undergraduate students. Scholars from the humanities, social sciences, law, media studies, political science, psychology, sociology, history, religious studies, and related fields are encouraged to apply. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit: • Cover letter • Curriculum vitae • Research statement • Names and addresses of three references Applications received by September 15, 2026 will receive full consideration, but the search will remain open until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted electronically at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33982 For questions about the position, please contact: Alvin H. Rosenfeld, Director, Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism at rosenfel@iu.edu . Basic Qualifications Basic Qualifications • Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field • An established record of significant scholarly achievement focused on antisemitism • Demonstrated expertise in contemporary manifestations of antisemitism • Evidence of sustained research productivity Department Contact for Questions Alvin H. Rosenfeld, Director, Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism at rosenfel@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Preferred Qualifications • Senior scholarly standing • Record of successful external grant funding • Demonstrated capacity for intellectual leadership and interdisciplinary collaboration • Experience mentoring graduate students and advanced undergraduate students. Scholars from the humanities, social sciences, law, media studies, political science, psychology, sociology, history, religious studies, and related fields are encouraged to apply. Salary and Rank Minimum salary, $75,000; final salary commensurate with rank, qualifications, and experience. Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 09/15/2026 Expected Start Date 01/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101625-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Statement of Research and Teaching List Of References Optional Documents Other Other 2 Other 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34922": {
      "title": "Public Management, Tenure Track Faculty Position",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Public Management, Tenure Track Faculty Position Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Public Management, Tenure Track Faculty Position Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Public Management, Tenure Track Faculty Position Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Public & Environmental Affairs Location Bloomington Position Summary PAUL H. O’NEILL SCHOOL OF PUBLIC AND ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS INDIANA UNIVERSITY - BLOOMINGTON CAMPUS Public Management, Tenure Track Faculty Position The O’Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University-Bloomington, a world leader in public affairs research and education, invites applications for an open rank tenure track faculty position in all major areas of research specialization within public management. Preferred start date is August 1, 2027. Junior applicants are expected to develop a record of successful teaching and mentoring of students from all backgrounds and a nationally competitive program of research, including external funding. Senior applicants should already have a well-established national and international reputation in their research area and be able to provide evidence of effective teaching and mentoring. Applicants should demonstrate the ability to contribute to O’Neill’s research programs, curricular offerings and service portfolio. Preferred start date is August 1, 2027. O’Neill Bloomington contains a vibrant student and faculty community and is the largest school of public affairs and public policy in the United States and for five years has ranked in one of the top two spots of the U.S. News & World Report rankings for graduate public affairs schools in the country. Six of O’Neill’s specialty programs are ranked in the top five listings nationally, including Nonprofit Management and Environmental Policy which are ranked number 1. O’Neill is also tied for the top-ranked public administration program according to the 2020 Academic Ranking of World Universities. The school attracts very high performing faculty and provides a collegial workplace environment. Indiana University is a major research University founded in 1820 and currently enrolls more than 38,000 undergraduates and 10,000 graduate and professional students on the Bloomington campus. Additional information about the school and Indiana University can be found at https://oneill.indiana.edu/ . Bloomington is a welcoming community located in the beautiful, wooded hills of southern Indiana approximately 1 hour from Indianapolis and 4 hours from Chicago, with abundant cultural and recreational opportunities, lower housing costs, and strong schools. Additional information about the city of Bloomington can be found at https://www.visitbloomington.com/ . To ensure consideration, application materials must be received by October 1, 2026. However, the search may continue until the position is filled. Please submit a letter of application, current vita, writing sample, and contact information for three references to https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33995 . Basic Qualifications Minimum educational qualifications include a Ph.D. upon appointment in a related field (e.g., public administration, public affairs, political science, public policy, psychology, sociology, or business administration) and evidence of a developing or developed research portfolio depending on rank. Department Contact for Questions Inquiries regarding the position should be sent to the chair of the search committee, Sean Nicholson-Crotty, seanicho@iu.edu. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101627-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Writing Sample List Of References Optional Documents Letter of Reference 1 Letter of Reference 2 Letter of Reference 3 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34910": {
      "title": "Roger E. Covey Professorship in Silk Road Studies (Open Rank, Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Roger E. Covey Professorship in Silk Road Studies (Open Rank, Tenure Track) Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Roger E. Covey Professorship in Silk Road Studies (Open Rank, Tenure Track) Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information Posting Details Position Details Title Roger E. Covey Professorship in Silk Road Studies (Open Rank, Tenure Track) Appointment Status Tenure Department IU Bloomington Central Eurasian Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Central Eurasian Studies in the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies at Indiana University Bloomington seeks to make a tenure-track or tenured hire (open rank) to fill the Roger E. Covey Professorship in Silk Road Studies. The successful candidate will contribute to the Department’s mission in the study of Central Eurasia and play a leading role in advancing Silk Road Studies across the Hamilton Lugar School and beyond. Applicants should have an established or emerging record of innovative research on the overland and/or maritime Silk Roads (2nd century BCE through 15th century CE) and their relevance to the contemporary world. We welcome applications from scholars whose research advances understanding of one or more aspects of the histories, societies, religions, languages, politics, diplomacy, material worlds, and interconnections of the overland and/or maritime Silk Roads. Candidates must demonstrate proficiency in one or more major languages necessary to conduct original research in their area of specialization, including the use of primary sources. Proficiency in one or more languages taught in or closely affiliated with the Department is also highly desirable. Additionally, applicants should demonstrate excellence in teaching in Silk Road-related Studies, and a commitment to public engagement and program development of Silk Road Studies within the Department as well as within the Hamilton Lugar School. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to teach classes appealing to a broad range of students at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Teaching will be conducted in English. Review of applications will begin immediately, with all applications received by September 30, 2026 being guaranteed full consideration. The appointment begins August 1, 2027. Required Application Materials: Application materials must include a cover letter, curriculum vitae/resume, career statement describing research, service, teaching experiences and contact information of three academic referees, samples of published, in-press, and forthcoming research works, and previous teaching materials and course evaluations. Applicant referees will receive an email link to submit their letters of reference directly to the search committee through the PeopleAdmin interface. Application materials may be submitted via the PeopleAdmin applicant portal at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34008 Basic Qualifications Candidates must have a PhD degree by the starting date of the appointment. Proficiency in one or more languages necessary to conduct original research in their area of specialization, including the use of primary sources (e.g., Turkic, Iranian, Central Asian, Sino-Tibetan languages) is required. Department Contact for Questions For questions regarding the Department of Central Eurasian Studies, please contact Ms. Jessica Smith, CEUS department manager at jsmith23@iu.edu or visit the departmental website at ceus.indiana.edu. For questions regarding the application submission process, please contact Mr. Zac Muller, Assistant Director of Faculty Relations, Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies, at zmuller@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Proficiency in one or more languages taught in or closely affiliated with the Department is also highly desirable. For applicants seeking appointment with tenure: Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Salary and Rank Special Instructions Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information For Best Consideration Date 09/30/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101630-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Evidence of Teaching Ability Publication 1 Sample Syllabus Statement of Research and Teaching Optional Documents Publication 2 Publication 3 Publication 4 Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34904": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Clinical Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Clinical Assistant Professor Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Required Application Materials – Please include all of the following : 1. Cover Letter: including explanation of why you are interested in this position and how your training and experience make you a good fit 2. CV 3. Graduate School Transcript (unofficial is sufficient) 4. Teaching Statement: including explanation of your teaching philosophy, examples of pedagogical strategies, and statement on how these relate to a clinical science curriculum 5. References: name, title, and contact information for three professional references OPTIONAL Application Materials – You may choose to include any or none of the following: 1. Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness: e.g., course evaluations; peer teaching evaluation; formal course observation, etc. 2. Example Syllabus 3. Example Course Materials: e.g., representative example lecture slides; lecture recording; and/or class assignments 4. Research Statement 5. Example Publication Application Deadline: For best consideration please submit your application by December 1, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. How to Apply: applicants should apply online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34662 Posting Details Position Details Title Clinical Assistant Professor Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Psychological & Brain Sciences Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences at Indiana University, Bloomington ( https://psych.indiana.edu/ ), invites applications for a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure-track position at the level of Clinical Assistant Professor to begin August 1, 2027. The qualified candidate will contribute to a new, highly innovative, and rigorous Bachelor of Science degree program in Clinical Psychological Science ( see: https://psych.indiana.edu/undergraduate/clinical-psych-bs.html ). This rapidly growing major is the first of its kind in the country and provides undergraduates with classroom and practicum-based training in experimental psychopathology and evidence-based assessment, intervention, dissemination, and implementation following a clinical science training model ( see: https://psych.indiana.edu/news-events/news/2025/clinical-science-major.html ). Job duties consist primarily of undergraduate teaching and may involve supervision of applied clinical experiences (e.g., internships & practica at local mental health and community-based organizations). We seek an individual with experience or ability to teach Research Methods in Clinical Science, Psychological Assessment and Testing, Abnormal Psychology, or evidence-based clinical interventions courses on disorders and topics within the candidate’s primary area of specialization. Candidates should be committed to a career of excellence in teaching and service, including coordinating degree requirements for the major, advising undergraduate students in academic and professional pursuits, and serving on departmental committees (e.g., student awards, undergraduate curricular planning). This will be a full-time non-tenure track, faculty appointment beginning with a 3-year renewable appointment, with promotion opportunities to Associate Clinical Professor and then Full Clinical Professor, based on demonstrated excellence in teaching and/or service. About Bloomington: Bloomington, Indiana is a university town with high quality of life and low cost of living. Bloomington is within a one-hour drive of Indianapolis and a two-hour drive of two other major metropolitan centers and four-hour drive of several others. IU Bloomington also has competitive entry-level faculty salaries. The campus is home to many thriving affinity groups and offers an exceptional selection of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities. Here ( https://psych.indiana.edu/about/employment/faculty-hiring/index.html ), you will find contact information for a range of people with knowledge of the hiring process, as well as department, campus, and general life in Bloomington, who have volunteered to be search advocates and are not on the search committee. If you have questions that you may not feel comfortable bringing to the department chair or the members of the search committee, we encourage you to reach out to these faculty. Basic Qualifications Minimum qualifications are a Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology or a closely related discipline (e.g., Counseling, School Psychology) at the time of the appointment August 1, 2027 and undergraduate teaching experience. Department Contact for Questions Queries can be sent to Dr. Lorenzo Lorenzo-Luaces, Search Committee Chair, ATTN : Clinical Science Search, Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, 1101 E. 10th Street, Bloomington, IN 47405-7007 or dctpbs@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Required Application Materials – Please include all of the following : 1. Cover Letter: including explanation of why you are interested in this position and how your training and experience make you a good fit 2. CV 3. Graduate School Transcript (unofficial is sufficient) 4. Teaching Statement: including explanation of your teaching philosophy, examples of pedagogical strategies, and statement on how these relate to a clinical science curriculum 5. References: name, title, and contact information for three professional references OPTIONAL Application Materials – You may choose to include any or none of the following: 1. Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness: e.g., course evaluations; peer teaching evaluation; formal course observation, etc. 2. Example Syllabus 3. Example Course Materials: e.g., representative example lecture slides; lecture recording; and/or class assignments 4. Research Statement 5. Example Publication Application Deadline: For best consideration please submit your application by December 1, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. How to Apply: applicants should apply online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34662 For Best Consideration Date 12/01/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101740-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy List Of References Transcript Optional Documents Evidence of Teaching Ability Other Publication 1 Sample Syllabus Statement of Research and Teaching Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34902": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate or Full Professor, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant or Associate or Full Professor, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant or Associate or Full Professor, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant or Associate or Full Professor, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Criminology and Criminal Justice Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice at Indiana University, Bloomington invites applications for one open rank position, tenure-track or tenured, beginning the fall of 2027. We seek an individual with expertise in criminology and/or criminal justice with a specialization in victimology. Candidates must have a PhD in hand by the start date of the appointment; current ABDs are welcome to apply. Applicants’ degree must be in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a closely related social science discipline. Applicants with only a law degree (JD, LLM , SJD ) will not be considered. We seek an individual committed to excellence in both scholarship and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The teaching load is two courses per semester. Applicants should demonstrate the ability to maintain an active research agenda, mentor and supervise students, and provide service to the Department, University, and academic community. The Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice, housed in the College of Arts & Sciences, has a long and distinguished record of nationally recognized scholarship, teaching, and service, offering degrees at the bachelors, masters, and PhD levels. Its graduate program currently has 24 research active PhD students, and its undergraduate program routinely has around 700 majors. The department is one of the oldest in the country with an established interdisciplinary history. Faculty members are involved in a wide array of research, funded by major public agencies and private foundations, while also working closely with leading state and local criminal justice agencies. For more information about the Department, please visit the department’s website at https://criminaljustice.indiana.edu/ . Indiana University has nearly 90,000 students enrolled in more than 980 academic programs across nine campuses. IU Bloomington, the flagship campus, offers over 500 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. Founded in 1820, the university is designated as a Carnegie R1 institution, providing extensive support for both internal and external grants, teaching, and international collaborations. Applicants should apply online at https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34406 including (1) a cover letter outlining the applicant’s qualifications, and research and teaching goals; (2) a complete curriculum vitae; and (3) contact information for three letters of reference. Review of applications will begin October 23rd, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to Dr. Adam Ghazi-Tehrani, Search Committee Chair, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice, 1033 E. 3rd St., 346 Sycamore Hall, Bloomington, IN 47405-7005 or aghazite@iu.edu . Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct seriously. Basic Qualifications We seek an individual with expertise in criminology and/or criminal justice with a specialization in victimology. Candidates must have a PhD in hand by the start date of the appointment; current ABDs are welcome to apply. Applicants’ degree must be in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a closely related social science discipline. Applicants with only a law degree (JD, LLM , SJD ) will not be considered. Department Contact for Questions Questions regarding the position or application process can be directed to Dr. Adam Ghazi-Tehrani, Search Committee Chair, Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice, 1033 E. 3rd St., 346 Sycamore Hall, Bloomington, IN 47405-7005 or aghazite@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 10/23/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101697-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No How did you hear about this position? Personal Contact: At Professional Meeting or Conference Personal Contact: Direct Contact by Search Committee Personal Contact: Referred by colleague or advisor Announcement: Chronicle of Higher Education Announcement: IU Bulletin for Academic Appointees Announcement: Other Journal or Magazine Announcement: Other Website Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34900": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Astronomy",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Postdoctoral Researcher, Astronomy Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Postdoctoral Researcher, Astronomy Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications, including a CV, publication list, statement of research experience and plans, and three letters of recommendation should be submitted through the application portal: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34885 Applications and letters of recommendation must be received by December 7, 2026 to receive full consideration. Questions about the application process can also be sent to astdept@iu.edu. Posting Details Position Details Title Postdoctoral Researcher, Astronomy Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Astronomy Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of Astronomy at Indiana University invites applications for the Sullivan Prize Postdoctoral Fellowship to begin in the Fall of 2027. The search is open to applicants from any area of astronomy or astrophysics; we are particularly interested in candidates whose research complements and enhances current department strengths and who will collaborate directly with department faculty. The department’s research activity includes observational, theoretical, and computational projects in Galactic and extra-galactic astrophysics, stellar astrophysics, exoplanets, and stellar dynamics (see the Department’s website https://astro.indiana.edu for further information). The successful applicant will have access to observing time on the WIYN 3.5-m telescope at Kitt Peak (including the NEID high-precision radial velocity spectrometer), and on other telescope facilities to which the department has access. They will also have direct use of the outstanding IU information technology infrastructure, including full access to various supercomputers and massive data storage systems (see here for further details https://uits.iu.edu/services/technology-for-research/high-performance-computing-and-storage ). The department has a supportive and collegial atmosphere. We maintain close ties with the Physics Department and the wider campus community. IU-Bloomington is the flagship campus of the IU system and is centrally located in a community of ~80,000 people with an outstanding public park system and a vibrant fine arts, theater, and entertainment scene. This position is a full time, benefits-eligible position with a salary of $70,000 per year and additional funds for research expenses. The initial appointment will be for two years with extension to the third year depending on satisfactory performance. Basic Qualifications Minimum qualifications: Ph.D. in Astronomy, Astrophysics, or Physics by appointment start date. Department Contact for Questions astdept@iu.edu Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions Applications, including a CV, publication list, statement of research experience and plans, and three letters of recommendation should be submitted through the application portal: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34885 Applications and letters of recommendation must be received by December 7, 2026 to receive full consideration. Questions about the application process can also be sent to astdept@iu.edu. For Best Consideration Date 12/07/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101765-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Other Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34948": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Global Production and Trade Governance",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Assistant/Associate Professor of Global Production and Trade Governance Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Assistant/Associate Professor of Global Production and Trade Governance Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information Posting Details Position Details Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Global Production and Trade Governance Appointment Status Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington International Studies Location Bloomington Position Summary The Department of International Studies at Indiana University’s Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies invites applications for an Assistant/Associate Professor, tenure-track faculty position in Global Production and Trade Governance beginning August 1, 2027. We seek a scholar whose research and teaching examine the governance, law, and political economy of global production systems. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, global value chains, international trade and investment politics, the regulation of transnational production networks, foreign direct investment, and the implications of global economic governance for development and inequality. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s growing strengths in international political economy and global governance while supporting existing undergraduate and graduate programs, including the International Financial Governance, Trade, and Development degree and the finance and trade concentration within the Master of International Affairs ( MIA ) program. The position reflects rising student and institutional interest in issues surrounding globalization, economic interdependence, financial governance, and the evolving rules and institutions that shape international economic relations. We welcome applications from candidates employing diverse disciplinary and methodological approaches. Candidates should demonstrate a strong research agenda, evidence of or potential for excellence in teaching, and a commitment to interdisciplinary engagement within an international studies environment. Responsibilities include maintaining an active research program, teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in areas related to global production and trade governance, mentoring students, and contributing to departmental and university service. Applicants seeking appointment at associate rank with tenure will be required to undergo a review of appropriateness for hire with tenure at Indiana University as a part of the recruitment process. Applicants seeking appointment at assistant rank must have a doctoral degree in hand by August 1, 2027. Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, a sample course syllabus, writing sample, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin following a priority deadline of September 11, 2026, with all applications received by that date being guaranteed full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Interested candidates should review the application requirements and submit their materials via the PeopleAdmin applicant portal at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/33720 Basic Qualifications Ph.D. in International Studies, Political Science, Political Economy, Economics, Law, Geography, Sociology, or a related field by the time of appointment; Record of scholarly achievement commensurate with appointed rank; evidence of teaching excellence or strong potential for effective teaching Department Contact for Questions Inquiries regarding the position, the Department of International Studies, and the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies may be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Sarah Bauerle Danzman at sbauerle@iu.edu . Questions regarding technical aspects of the application process may be directed to Mr. Zac Muller, Hamilton Lugar School Assistant Director of Faculty Relations, at zmuller@iu.edu . Additional Qualifications For applicants seeking appointment with tenure: Before a conditional offer of employment with tenure is finalized, candidates will be asked to disclose any pending investigations or previous findings of sexual or professional misconduct. They will also be required to authorize an inquiry by Indiana University Bloomington with all current and former employers along these lines. The relevance of information disclosed or ascertained in the context of this process to a candidate’s eligibility for hire will be evaluated by Indiana University Bloomington on a case-by-case basis. Applicants should be aware, however, that Indiana University Bloomington takes the matters of sexual and professional misconduct very seriously. Salary and Rank Special Instructions Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services, and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information For Best Consideration Date 09/11/2026 Expected Start Date 08/01/2027 Posting Number IU-101601-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application Statement of Teaching Philosophy Sample Syllabus Research Agenda Writing Sample Optional Documents Other Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34941": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology; Gandara Lab",
      "description": "Indiana University Academic Positions | Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology; Gandara Lab Skip to Main Content Academic Careers at IU Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Campus IU Bloomington>> IU Indianapolis>> IU East>> IU Kokomo>> IU Northwest>> IU South Bend>> IU Southeast>> IU Columbus>> IU Fort Wayne>> IU School of Medicine>> Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology; Gandara Lab Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, containing policy statements, crime and fire statistics for all Indiana University campuses, is available online . You may also request a physical copy by emailing IU Public Safety at iups@iu.edu Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Details Title Research Associate/Lab Manager Biology; Gandara Lab Appointment Status Non-Tenure Track Department IU Bloomington Biology Location Bloomington Position Summary The department of Biology is a large, unified department with strong undergraduate degrees, nationally-ranked graduate programs, and world-class research spanning the breadth of biological questions and experimental systems — from ecosystems to microbiology and developmental biology, from evolution to cell biology, from molecular biology to systems biology, bioinformatics, and genomics. It is always an exciting time for Biology — enormous advances in global genome analysis coupled with unprecedented developments in interdisciplinary research have made the 21st century the Century of Biology. For more information about the department, you can find it here: About: Department of Biology: Indiana University Bloomington . Indiana University is an equal opportunity employer and provider of ADA services and prohibits discrimination in hiring. See Indiana University Notice of Non-Discrimination here which includes contact information. The Gandara Lab in the Department of Biology at Indiana University Bloomington is seeking a Research Associate/Lab Manager. About the lab Our lab takes an integrative physiology approach to investigate the genetic basis, molecular mechanisms, and physiological processes that allow animals to respond to chemical stress. Working with Drosophila flies and other insects, we use a broad range of high-throughput phenotyping techniques to determine the full impact of chemical stressors (agrochemicals, pollutants, etc.) on these animals. By combining these methods with genetic and pharmacological manipulations, we aim to understand the mechanisms underlying the chemical stress response and how they interact with the rest of the animal’s physiology. About the role We are looking for a motivated, curious person who wants to work in a highly collaborative and supportive research environment. In this role, you will assist lab members with research protocols and general lab tasks, contribute to discussions, and offer feedback and suggestions during lab meetings and journal clubs. Core duties 1. Manage general lab operations 2. Learn, perform, and teach other lab members our key research protocols: molecular biology techniques, fluorescence microscopy (wide-field, confocal, etc.), behavioral assays, fly genetics, and data analysis in R 3. Collect data following established protocols 4. Oversee research compliance, lab safety, permits, and associated documentation Other responsibilities 1. Test lab equipment and establish SOPs 2. Write research reports and present findings at lab meetings 3. Troubleshoot technical problems in the lab Basic Qualifications Required education and experience Bachelor’s degree and two years of research experience, OR a Master’s degree that includes research experience Required skills · Ability to work effectively as part of a team · Efficient time management · Professionalism · Organization and attention to detail · Strong written and oral communication · Ability to anticipate and solve problems · Dependability and initiative Preferred skills · Hands-on research experience with flies · Proficiency in one or more molecular biology or imaging techniques · Proficiency in R or Python Department Contact for Questions How to apply Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your research and educational background, career goals, interest in this position, and the date you are available to start; (2) a CV or résumé; and (3) names and contact information for two references. Apply at: https://indiana.peopleadmin.com/postings/34937 . Questions about the position can be directed to Lautaro Gandara (lgandara@iu.edu). Timing and compensation Review of applications will begin November 23 and continue until the position is filled. The ideal start date is January 4, 2027. The initial appointment is for one year, with reappointment contingent on satisfactory performance. Salary is commensurate with experience, and full benefits are included. The best consideration date is 11/23. Additional Qualifications Salary and Rank Special Instructions For Best Consideration Date 11/23/2027 Expected Start Date Posting Number IU-101768-2026 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you previously been employed with IU? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Letter of Application List Of References Optional Documents Copyright © 2026 The Trustees of Indiana University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
  },
